const topics = [
  {
    id: 'core-java',
    name: 'Core Java & OOP',
    desc: 'Fundamentals, OOP principles, language internals',
    questions: [
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between JDK, JRE, and JVM?',
        a: '<strong>JVM</strong> (Java Virtual Machine) executes bytecode and is platform-specific. <strong>JRE</strong> (Java Runtime Environment) = JVM + core libraries needed to run Java apps. <strong>JDK</strong> (Java Development Kit) = JRE + development tools (<code>javac</code>, <code>jar</code>, <code>javadoc</code>). You need the JDK to compile, only the JRE to run.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'Explain the four pillars of OOP.',
        a: '<ul><li><strong>Encapsulation</strong> — bundling data with methods that operate on it; hiding internal state via access modifiers.</li><li><strong>Inheritance</strong> — child classes derive properties/behavior from parent (<code>extends</code>).</li><li><strong>Polymorphism</strong> — same interface, different implementations (method overriding / overloading).</li><li><strong>Abstraction</strong> — exposing only essential features via abstract classes or interfaces.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between == and .equals() in Java?',
        a: '<code>==</code> compares <strong>references</strong> (memory addresses) for objects, or primitive values directly. <code>.equals()</code> compares <strong>content/state</strong> — but only if properly overridden. Default <code>Object.equals()</code> behaves like <code>==</code>. Always override <code>equals()</code> and <code>hashCode()</code> together.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between abstract class and interface?',
        a: 'Abstract classes can have constructors, instance fields, and concrete methods; a class can extend only one. Interfaces support multiple inheritance, are stateless (only constants), and since Java 8 support <code>default</code> and <code>static</code> methods, since Java 9 <code>private</code> methods. Use abstract class for "is-a" with shared state; interface for capability/contract.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How does the String pool work? What\'s the difference between String literal and new String()?',
        a: 'String literals are interned in the <strong>String Pool</strong> (part of heap since Java 7). <code>String a = "hello"</code> reuses the pooled instance; <code>String b = new String("hello")</code> always creates a new heap object. Call <code>.intern()</code> to force pool lookup. This matters for memory and for <code>==</code> behavior.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain method overloading vs. overriding, and what cannot be overloaded.',
        a: '<strong>Overloading</strong> = same method name, different parameter list (compile-time polymorphism). <strong>Overriding</strong> = subclass redefines parent method with identical signature (runtime polymorphism). You cannot overload based solely on <strong>return type</strong> or <strong>throws clause</strong>. Static methods are hidden, not overridden.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'Why are String objects immutable in Java? List at least three reasons.',
        a: '<ul><li><strong>Security</strong> — used in classloading, file paths, network connections; mutability would allow tampering after validation.</li><li><strong>Caching</strong> — hashCode is cached because it can never change; enables string pool.</li><li><strong>Thread safety</strong> — immutable objects are inherently thread-safe.</li><li><strong>Class loading</strong> — class names passed as strings; mutation could load the wrong class.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'Explain the equals/hashCode contract and what breaks when it\'s violated.',
        a: 'Contract: (1) equal objects must have equal hashCodes; (2) unequal objects <em>should</em> have different hashCodes (not required, but improves performance); (3) both must be consistent. Violation breaks <code>HashMap</code>, <code>HashSet</code>: object stored in bucket X but searched in bucket Y → "lost" objects. Always override both together.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What is the difference between checked and unchecked exceptions? When would you create a custom one?',
        a: '<strong>Checked</strong> (extends <code>Exception</code>): compiler enforces handling — for recoverable conditions (<code>IOException</code>). <strong>Unchecked</strong> (extends <code>RuntimeException</code>): for programming errors (<code>NullPointerException</code>, <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>). Modern Spring/REST style favors unchecked for cleaner code. Create custom when you need to carry domain-specific context for error handlers.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How does Java handle multiple inheritance, and what is the diamond problem solution since Java 8?',
        a: 'Java disallows multiple class inheritance to avoid the diamond problem. With Java 8 default methods in interfaces, the diamond can recur. Rules: (1) class wins over interface; (2) more specific interface wins; (3) if ambiguous, the implementing class <strong>must</strong> override and can call <code>Interface.super.method()</code> explicitly.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between final, finally, and finalize?',
        a: '<code>final</code> — modifier: a final class can\'t be subclassed, a final method can\'t be overridden, a final variable can\'t be reassigned. <code>finally</code> — block that always runs after a <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> (used for cleanup). <code>finalize()</code> — deprecated since Java 9, removed in Java 18; the GC used to call it before reclaiming an object — use try-with-resources or <code>Cleaner</code> instead.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What does the static keyword do?',
        a: '<code>static</code> belongs to the class, not instances. Used for: <strong>fields</strong> (one copy shared across instances), <strong>methods</strong> (called without an instance, can\'t use <code>this</code>), <strong>nested classes</strong> (no enclosing-instance reference), and <strong>initializer blocks</strong> (run once when the class is loaded).' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What are autoboxing and unboxing?',
        a: 'Automatic conversion between primitive types and their wrapper classes. <code>Integer x = 5;</code> auto-boxes; <code>int y = x;</code> auto-unboxes. Pitfalls: <code>NullPointerException</code> if unboxing <code>null</code>, performance cost in tight loops, and confusing identity comparisons (<code>Integer</code> caches -128..127, so <code>==</code> works for small values but fails for large ones).' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between primitive types and wrapper classes?',
        a: 'Primitives (<code>int</code>, <code>boolean</code>, <code>double</code>, etc.) live on the stack, have no methods, can\'t be <code>null</code>, and are passed by value. Wrappers (<code>Integer</code>, <code>Boolean</code>, <code>Double</code>) are objects on the heap, can be <code>null</code>, work with generics and collections, but cost more memory and indirection.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What are the rules for constructors in Java?',
        a: 'Same name as the class, no return type (not even <code>void</code>). Can be overloaded. If you don\'t declare any, the compiler adds a no-arg default. Subclasses must call a parent constructor as the first statement — implicitly <code>super()</code> or explicitly <code>super(args)</code>. Can be <code>private</code> (used for singletons/factories). Cannot be <code>abstract</code>, <code>static</code>, or <code>final</code>.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between String, StringBuilder, and StringBuffer?',
        a: '<code>String</code> — immutable; every modification creates a new object. <code>StringBuilder</code> — mutable, <strong>not thread-safe</strong>, faster; default choice for building strings. <code>StringBuffer</code> — mutable and <strong>synchronized</strong> (thread-safe), slower; rarely needed today. Concatenating with <code>+</code> in a loop is O(n²) — use a builder.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What are the key methods of the Object class?',
        a: '<ul><li><code>equals()</code>, <code>hashCode()</code> — identity/equality.</li><li><code>toString()</code> — default is class@hash; override for readability.</li><li><code>getClass()</code> — runtime class for reflection.</li><li><code>clone()</code> — shallow copy (Cloneable marker required).</li><li><code>wait()</code>, <code>notify()</code>, <code>notifyAll()</code> — intrinsic-lock coordination.</li><li><code>finalize()</code> — deprecated; don\'t use.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What can Java enums do beyond plain constants?',
        a: 'Enums can have <strong>fields, constructors, and methods</strong>; each constant can override methods to give per-value behavior (a strategy pattern in one declaration). They implicitly extend <code>java.lang.Enum</code>, so they get <code>name()</code>, <code>ordinal()</code>, <code>values()</code>, <code>valueOf()</code>. <code>EnumSet</code> and <code>EnumMap</code> are bit-vector and array-backed — extremely fast. Enums are also the simplest singleton implementation.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is a marker interface? Name a few examples.',
        a: 'An empty interface that signals a capability or contract to the JVM, framework, or tooling without declaring methods. Examples: <code>Serializable</code> (eligible for serialization), <code>Cloneable</code> (allows <code>Object.clone()</code>), <code>RandomAccess</code> (signals O(1) random access for collections). Modern style prefers annotations for the same purpose.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What are varargs and how do they interact with overloading?',
        a: '<code>method(String... args)</code> accepts zero or more arguments — the compiler packs them into a <code>String[]</code>. Must be the last parameter. <strong>Overloading pitfalls</strong>: exact matches and fixed-arity overloads win over varargs; <code>method(null)</code> is ambiguous if both <code>String</code> and <code>String...</code> overloads exist. Don\'t mix <code>Object...</code> with autoboxing — the resulting array contents may surprise you.' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'collections',
    name: 'Collections & Generics',
    desc: 'Lists, maps, sets, type erasure, internal mechanics',
    questions: [
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between ArrayList and LinkedList?',
        a: '<code>ArrayList</code>: backed by dynamic array, O(1) random access, O(n) insert/remove in middle. <code>LinkedList</code>: doubly-linked nodes, O(n) random access, O(1) add/remove at ends. ArrayList wins for most use cases due to CPU cache locality; LinkedList rarely outperforms it in practice.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'When would you use HashSet vs. TreeSet vs. LinkedHashSet?',
        a: '<code>HashSet</code> — unordered, O(1) operations, fastest. <code>LinkedHashSet</code> — preserves insertion order, slight memory overhead. <code>TreeSet</code> — sorted (natural order or Comparator), O(log n), backed by Red-Black tree. Pick by ordering need.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between List, Set, and Map?',
        a: '<code>List</code> — ordered, allows duplicates, index-based. <code>Set</code> — no duplicates, generally unordered. <code>Map</code> — key→value pairs, keys unique. Map is not part of <code>Collection</code> hierarchy.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain how HashMap works internally.',
        a: 'Backed by an array of buckets (default 16). Key\'s <code>hashCode()</code> determines bucket via <code>(n-1) & hash</code>. Collisions are stored in a linked list within the bucket; since Java 8, when a bucket exceeds 8 entries (and table ≥64), it converts to a <strong>balanced tree</strong> (Red-Black) — O(log n) instead of O(n). Resizes when load factor (default 0.75) is exceeded.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Why must HashMap keys override equals() and hashCode()?',
        a: '<code>hashCode()</code> selects the bucket; <code>equals()</code> resolves collisions within that bucket. If two equal keys have different hashCodes, they go to different buckets and the second insert becomes a duplicate. If hashCodes match but equals returns false inconsistently, lookups fail. This is why immutable keys are strongly recommended.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between fail-fast and fail-safe iterators?',
        a: '<strong>Fail-fast</strong> (most <code>java.util</code> — ArrayList, HashMap): throw <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> if collection is modified during iteration via mod-count tracking. <strong>Fail-safe</strong> (<code>java.util.concurrent</code> — CopyOnWriteArrayList, ConcurrentHashMap): iterate over a snapshot or with weak consistency, no exception but may miss recent updates.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'Explain type erasure and its implications.',
        a: 'Generics are a compile-time feature. The compiler replaces type parameters with their bounds (usually <code>Object</code>) and inserts casts. At runtime, <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> and <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> are both just <code>List</code>. Implications: cannot do <code>new T[]</code>, cannot use <code>instanceof T</code>, cannot overload methods that erase to the same signature, and reflection cannot see generic types from instances.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'Explain PECS — Producer Extends, Consumer Super.',
        a: 'For a parameterized type T: use <code>&lt;? extends T&gt;</code> when the collection <strong>produces</strong> T (you read from it); use <code>&lt;? super T&gt;</code> when the collection <strong>consumes</strong> T (you write to it). Example: <code>Collections.copy(List&lt;? super T&gt; dest, List&lt;? extends T&gt; src)</code>. This maximizes API flexibility while preserving type safety.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How does ConcurrentHashMap achieve thread safety without locking the whole map?',
        a: 'Java 7 used segment-based locking (16 segments, each with its own lock). Java 8 replaced segments with a single array using <strong>CAS</strong> operations for insertion and <strong>synchronized blocks on the bucket head</strong> for collision handling. Reads are typically lock-free. Tree-bin conversion preserved. Result: much higher concurrency than <code>Hashtable</code> or <code>Collections.synchronizedMap</code>.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What is the difference between Comparable and Comparator? When to use each?',
        a: '<code>Comparable&lt;T&gt;</code> defines the <strong>natural ordering</strong> via <code>compareTo()</code> — single ordering baked into the class. <code>Comparator&lt;T&gt;</code> is an external strategy — multiple orderings, no source access needed, composable via <code>thenComparing()</code>. Modern code uses Comparator lambdas: <code>Comparator.comparing(User::getName).reversed()</code>.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between Collection and Collections?',
        a: '<code>Collection</code> — the root interface of the collection hierarchy (parent of <code>List</code>, <code>Set</code>, <code>Queue</code>). <code>Collections</code> (with the s) — utility class with static methods like <code>sort()</code>, <code>reverse()</code>, <code>unmodifiableList()</code>, <code>synchronizedMap()</code>, <code>emptyList()</code>.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you create an immutable list or map in Java 9+?',
        a: 'Factory methods: <code>List.of(a, b, c)</code>, <code>Set.of(...)</code>, <code>Map.of(k1, v1, k2, v2)</code>, <code>Map.ofEntries(Map.entry(...), ...)</code>. They reject <code>null</code> and throw <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code> on modification. Use <code>List.copyOf(other)</code> to snapshot an existing collection as immutable.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between Iterator and Iterable?',
        a: '<code>Iterable&lt;T&gt;</code> — anything you can iterate over (one method: <code>iterator()</code>). Required for the enhanced <code>for</code> loop. <code>Iterator&lt;T&gt;</code> — the cursor itself: <code>hasNext()</code>, <code>next()</code>, optional <code>remove()</code>. You implement <code>Iterable</code> on collections; you use <code>Iterator</code> to step through them.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'When would you use Queue or Deque?',
        a: '<code>Queue</code> — FIFO; use for tasks waiting in line. <code>Deque</code> (double-ended) — adds operations at both ends; can be used as a stack (<code>push</code>/<code>pop</code>) or queue. Prefer <code>ArrayDeque</code> over the legacy <code>Stack</code> (which extends <code>Vector</code>, is synchronized, and slow).' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How does ArrayList grow internally?',
        a: 'Default initial capacity is 10. When full, it grows to <code>oldCapacity + oldCapacity / 2</code> (1.5×) — copying all elements into a new array. If you know the size up front, pass a capacity to the constructor (<code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(10000)</code>) to avoid the repeated copies.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between HashMap, LinkedHashMap, and TreeMap?',
        a: '<code>HashMap</code> — unordered, O(1) average, allows one <code>null</code> key. <code>LinkedHashMap</code> — preserves insertion order (or access order if configured — useful for LRU caches). <code>TreeMap</code> — keys sorted by natural order or <code>Comparator</code>, O(log n) operations, implements <code>NavigableMap</code> (floor/ceiling/headMap).' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you safely remove elements while iterating a collection?',
        a: 'Three safe options: (1) <code>Iterator.remove()</code> while looping (avoids <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>); (2) <code>collection.removeIf(predicate)</code> — Java 8+, concise; (3) collect into a new list and replace. Never call <code>list.remove()</code> inside a <code>for-each</code> — it triggers a CME on the next iteration.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What are EnumMap and EnumSet, and why use them?',
        a: 'Specialized collections keyed by enum constants. <code>EnumMap</code> uses a backing array indexed by <code>ordinal()</code>; <code>EnumSet</code> uses a bit vector (a <code>long</code> for ≤64 values). Both are dramatically faster and smaller than <code>HashMap</code>/<code>HashSet</code> for enum keys. Iteration order is the enum declaration order.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between Vector, Stack, and ArrayDeque?',
        a: '<code>Vector</code> — legacy synchronized <code>List</code>, slow, avoid. <code>Stack</code> — extends <code>Vector</code>; inherits its issues. <code>ArrayDeque</code> — modern resizable-array double-ended queue, NOT thread-safe, but much faster as both a stack and a queue. Use <code>ArrayDeque</code> in single-threaded code; <code>ConcurrentLinkedDeque</code> for concurrent use.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between Collections.unmodifiableList() and List.copyOf()?',
        a: '<code>unmodifiableList()</code> returns a <strong>view</strong> — the underlying list can still be mutated and the view reflects it. <code>List.copyOf()</code> (Java 10+) returns a true <strong>immutable copy</strong> — independent of the source. Prefer <code>List.copyOf()</code> for defensive copies; use the view only when you intentionally want the original to drive updates.' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'multithreading',
    name: 'Multithreading & Concurrency',
    desc: 'Threads, locks, executors, memory model',
    questions: [
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between a process and a thread?',
        a: 'A <strong>process</strong> is an independent program with its own memory space. A <strong>thread</strong> is a unit of execution within a process, sharing heap memory but having its own stack. Threads are lighter to create and switch between, but require synchronization for shared state.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// Two threads share heap memory (the array)\nint[] counter = {0};\nRunnable task = () -&gt; counter[0]++;\nnew Thread(task).start();\nnew Thread(task).start();\n// counter[0] may be 1 or 2 — race on shared state</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What are the ways to create a thread in Java?',
        a: '<ul><li><code>extends Thread</code> — limited because Java has single inheritance.</li><li><code>implements Runnable</code> — preferred, separates task from worker.</li><li><code>Callable&lt;V&gt;</code> + <code>ExecutorService.submit()</code> — returns a result via <code>Future</code>.</li><li>Java 21 <code>Thread.ofVirtual().start(runnable)</code> — virtual threads.</li></ul><div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// Runnable\nnew Thread(() -&gt; System.out.println("hi")).start();\n\n// Callable + ExecutorService\nExecutorService ex = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();\nFuture&lt;Integer&gt; f = ex.submit(() -&gt; 42);\nint result = f.get();\n\n// Virtual thread (Java 21+)\nThread.ofVirtual().start(() -&gt; doWork());</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between synchronized method and synchronized block?',
        a: 'Synchronized method locks the <strong>entire</strong> object (instance for non-static, class object for static). Synchronized block locks only a specific section on a chosen monitor: <code>synchronized(lockObj) { ... }</code>. Blocks are more granular, reduce contention, and allow finer-grained locking strategies.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>class Counter {\n  private int count;\n  private final Object lock = new Object();\n\n  // Locks \'this\' for the whole method\n  public synchronized void incA() { count++; }\n\n  // Locks only the block, on a dedicated monitor\n  public void incB() {\n    synchronized (lock) { count++; }\n  }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the volatile keyword? When is it sufficient and when is it not?',
        a: '<code>volatile</code> guarantees <strong>visibility</strong> — writes are flushed to main memory, reads come from main memory — and prevents instruction reordering around it. It does <strong>not</strong> guarantee atomicity for compound operations (<code>i++</code> is read+modify+write). Sufficient for flags and single-writer patterns; use <code>AtomicInteger</code> or locks for compound operations.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// OK: single-writer visibility flag\nprivate volatile boolean running = true;\npublic void stop() { running = false; }\npublic void run() { while (running) { /* work */ } }\n\n// BROKEN: i++ is read-modify-write, not atomic\nprivate volatile int hits;\npublic void hit() { hits++; }   // race condition\n\n// FIX: AtomicInteger for compound ops\nprivate final AtomicInteger hits = new AtomicInteger();\npublic void hit() { hits.incrementAndGet(); }</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain the producer-consumer pattern using BlockingQueue.',
        a: '<code>BlockingQueue</code> (e.g., <code>ArrayBlockingQueue</code>, <code>LinkedBlockingQueue</code>) provides thread-safe handoff. Producers call <code>put()</code> which blocks if full; consumers call <code>take()</code> which blocks if empty. Eliminates manual wait/notify, prevents busy-waiting, and naturally backpressures producers when consumers are slow.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>BlockingQueue&lt;Task&gt; queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue&lt;&gt;(100);\n\n// Producer — blocks when full\nnew Thread(() -&gt; {\n  while (true) queue.put(nextTask());\n}).start();\n\n// Consumer — blocks when empty\nnew Thread(() -&gt; {\n  while (true) handle(queue.take());\n}).start();</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between ExecutorService and ForkJoinPool?',
        a: '<code>ExecutorService</code> (typically <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code>) runs independent tasks from a shared queue — best for I/O-bound or unrelated tasks. <code>ForkJoinPool</code> uses <strong>work-stealing</strong>: each thread has its own deque, idle threads steal from others — optimized for recursive divide-and-conquer (e.g., parallel streams).<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// ExecutorService — independent I/O tasks\nExecutorService io = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);\nio.submit(() -&gt; downloadFile(url));\n\n// ForkJoinPool — recursive divide-and-conquer\nclass SumTask extends RecursiveTask&lt;Long&gt; {\n  protected Long compute() {\n    if (small()) return computeDirect();\n    SumTask left  = new SumTask(lo, mid);\n    left.fork();                          // run async\n    SumTask right = new SumTask(mid, hi);\n    return right.compute() + left.join(); // steal-friendly\n  }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'Explain the Java Memory Model (JMM) and the happens-before relationship.',
        a: 'JMM defines how threads interact through memory. <strong>Happens-before</strong> guarantees that if action A happens-before B, A\'s effects are visible to B. Established by: program order within a thread, monitor lock release→acquire, volatile write→read, thread start/join, constructor completion→finalizer. Without happens-before, threads may observe stale or reordered writes.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>class SafePublish {\n  private Config config;\n  private volatile boolean ready;\n\n  // Thread A\n  public void init() {\n    config = loadConfig();   // write happens-before...\n    ready = true;            // ...this volatile write\n  }\n\n  // Thread B — reading \'ready\' establishes the edge\n  public Config get() {\n    return ready ? config : null;  // sees fully-built config\n  }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What are the differences between ReentrantLock and synchronized?',
        a: '<ul><li><code>ReentrantLock</code> supports <strong>tryLock</strong> (timeout), <strong>interruptible</strong> acquisition, <strong>fair</strong> mode, and multiple <code>Condition</code> objects.</li><li>Synchronized is JVM-level, simpler, auto-released on exception; ReentrantLock requires explicit <code>unlock()</code> in <code>finally</code>.</li><li>Performance is comparable since Java 6 due to biased/lightweight locking optimizations.</li></ul><div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();\n\n// tryLock with timeout — synchronized can\'t do this\nif (lock.tryLock(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {\n  try {\n    transfer(from, to, amount);\n  } finally {\n    lock.unlock();          // MUST be in finally\n  }\n} else {\n  log.warn("could not acquire lock in time");\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What is a deadlock? How do you prevent and detect it?',
        a: 'Deadlock requires four conditions: mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait. <strong>Prevention</strong>: acquire locks in a globally consistent order; use <code>tryLock()</code> with timeout; minimize lock scope; use lock-free structures. <strong>Detection</strong>: thread dump (<code>jstack</code>, <code>jconsole</code>) — JVM identifies cycles automatically. Watch for nested synchronized blocks across multiple objects.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// BAD: T1 locks a then b, T2 locks b then a → deadlock\nvoid transfer(Account a, Account b, int amt) {\n  synchronized (a) {\n    synchronized (b) { a.debit(amt); b.credit(amt); }\n  }\n}\n\n// GOOD: always lock by id order\nvoid transfer(Account a, Account b, int amt) {\n  Account first  = a.id() &lt; b.id() ? a : b;\n  Account second = a.id() &lt; b.id() ? b : a;\n  synchronized (first) {\n    synchronized (second) { a.debit(amt); b.credit(amt); }\n  }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What are virtual threads (Project Loom) and how do they differ from platform threads?',
        a: 'Virtual threads (stable in Java 21) are lightweight, JVM-managed threads multiplexed onto a small pool of platform (OS) threads via continuation-style suspension. Millions can coexist. They unblock when I/O is waiting, returning the carrier thread to the pool. Same <code>Thread</code> API, but much cheaper — kills the need for reactive frameworks in many I/O-heavy scenarios. Caveat: pinning via <code>synchronized</code> on I/O still blocks the carrier (improving in Java 22+).<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// Single virtual thread\nThread.ofVirtual().start(() -&gt; handle(request));\n\n// One virtual thread per task — millions are fine\ntry (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n  for (Request r : requests) {\n    ex.submit(() -&gt; blockingIoCall(r));\n  }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between Thread.sleep() and Object.wait()?',
        a: '<code>sleep()</code> pauses the current thread for a duration; it does NOT release any held locks. <code>wait()</code> must be called inside a <code>synchronized</code> block; it releases the monitor and waits to be notified (<code>notify</code>/<code>notifyAll</code>). Use <code>sleep()</code> for delays; use <code>wait()</code>/<code>notify()</code> for inter-thread coordination — or better, use higher-level concurrency tools.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is a daemon thread?',
        a: 'A background thread the JVM does not wait for at shutdown — when only daemon threads remain, the JVM exits. Set with <code>thread.setDaemon(true)</code> <em>before</em> start. Typical uses: GC, finalizer, monitoring, scheduled cleanup. Don\'t use for work that must complete (e.g., flushing a buffer to disk).' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What does Thread.join() do?',
        a: 'Blocks the caller until the joined thread finishes. <code>worker.join()</code> waits indefinitely; <code>worker.join(5000)</code> waits at most 5 seconds. Establishes a happens-before edge from the joined thread\'s last action to whatever runs after <code>join()</code> returns — so its results are safely visible.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is ThreadLocal and when would you use it?',
        a: 'A variable with a per-thread value — each thread reads/writes its own slot. Useful for per-request context (user ID, trace ID, JDBC connection in a transaction). <strong>Watch out</strong>: in thread pools, values persist across requests — always <code>remove()</code> in a <code>finally</code>. Also a common source of memory leaks in app servers.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between Runnable and Callable?',
        a: '<code>Runnable.run()</code> returns nothing and cannot throw checked exceptions. <code>Callable&lt;V&gt;.call()</code> returns a value (the <code>V</code>) and can throw checked exceptions. Submit a <code>Callable</code> to an <code>ExecutorService</code> to get a <code>Future&lt;V&gt;</code> back.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is CompletableFuture and how does it differ from Future?',
        a: '<code>Future</code> is a passive container — you poll <code>get()</code> and block. <code>CompletableFuture</code> is composable: <code>thenApply</code>, <code>thenCompose</code> (flat-map), <code>thenCombine</code> (zip), <code>exceptionally</code>, <code>allOf</code>/<code>anyOf</code>. Lets you build non-blocking pipelines. Always pass an explicit executor — the default uses the common <code>ForkJoinPool</code> which is shared and small.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Compare CountDownLatch, CyclicBarrier, and Semaphore.',
        a: '<ul><li><code>CountDownLatch</code> — one-shot; threads wait until the counter hits zero. Good for "wait until N tasks done".</li><li><code>CyclicBarrier</code> — reusable; N threads wait at a barrier, all proceed together. Good for staged parallel computations.</li><li><code>Semaphore</code> — pool of N permits; <code>acquire</code>/<code>release</code>. Used to cap concurrency on a resource.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is AtomicInteger and what is CAS?',
        a: '<code>AtomicInteger</code> wraps an <code>int</code> with lock-free, thread-safe operations like <code>incrementAndGet()</code>, <code>compareAndSet(expected, new)</code>. Built on <strong>CAS</strong> (Compare-And-Swap) — a CPU instruction that atomically updates a memory location if its current value matches an expected value. Faster than locks under low/medium contention; can degrade under heavy contention (the "CAS spin" problem).' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between ReadWriteLock and ReentrantLock?',
        a: '<code>ReentrantLock</code> is exclusive — one holder at a time. <code>ReadWriteLock</code> (typically <code>ReentrantReadWriteLock</code>) allows <strong>many concurrent readers</strong> or one writer. Big win when reads dominate. <code>StampedLock</code> (Java 8+) adds an optimistic-read mode that\'s even faster — but it\'s non-reentrant and trickier to use correctly.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What are the Executors factory methods, and what are their pitfalls?',
        a: '<ul><li><code>newFixedThreadPool(n)</code> — fixed size, <strong>unbounded queue</strong> — risk of OOM under sustained load.</li><li><code>newCachedThreadPool()</code> — unbounded thread count — risk of thread explosion on bursts.</li><li><code>newSingleThreadExecutor()</code> — same unbounded-queue risk as fixed.</li><li><code>newScheduledThreadPool(n)</code> — for periodic tasks.</li></ul>For production, build a <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> directly with a bounded queue and a sensible rejection policy.' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'jvm',
    name: 'JVM Internals & Performance',
    desc: 'Memory model, GC, classloading, tuning',
    questions: [
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What are the main memory areas of the JVM?',
        a: '<ul><li><strong>Heap</strong> — objects, shared across threads. Divided into Young (Eden, S0, S1) and Old generations.</li><li><strong>Stack</strong> — per-thread, method frames, local variables.</li><li><strong>Metaspace</strong> — class metadata (replaced PermGen in Java 8), native memory.</li><li><strong>PC register</strong> — per-thread, current instruction.</li><li><strong>Native method stack</strong> — for JNI calls.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between stack and heap memory?',
        a: 'Stack is per-thread, holds primitives and references, automatically reclaimed when method exits. Heap is shared across threads, holds objects, reclaimed by the garbage collector. Stack is fast (LIFO, contiguous); heap is slower but flexible. <code>StackOverflowError</code> vs <code>OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space</code>.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What does the garbage collector actually do?',
        a: 'GC reclaims memory by identifying unreachable objects from <strong>GC roots</strong> (local vars on stack, static fields, JNI references, active threads). Anything reachable is live; anything else is collectable. The collector then frees space and (often) compacts to reduce fragmentation.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain the generational hypothesis and how it shapes GC design.',
        a: 'Most objects die young. So JVM splits heap into Young Gen (Eden + 2 Survivor spaces) and Old Gen. Allocations go to Eden; survivors of a Minor GC are copied to a Survivor space and aged. After enough cycles they get <strong>tenured</strong> to Old Gen. Major/Full GC cleans Old Gen — slower and rarer. This makes Minor GC extremely fast since most Eden is dead.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Compare G1, ZGC, and Shenandoah.',
        a: '<ul><li><strong>G1</strong> (default since Java 9): region-based, predictable pause targets, good for medium heaps. Concurrent marking, parallel evacuation.</li><li><strong>ZGC</strong>: sub-millisecond pauses, scales to TB heaps, colored pointers, concurrent everything. Slightly higher throughput cost.</li><li><strong>Shenandoah</strong> (Red Hat): also ultra-low pause via concurrent compaction with Brooks pointers. Similar niche to ZGC.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What are strong, soft, weak, and phantom references?',
        a: '<strong>Strong</strong> — normal reference, prevents GC. <strong>Soft</strong> — collected only when JVM is near OOM; good for memory-sensitive caches. <strong>Weak</strong> — collected at the next GC if no strong refs exist (used in <code>WeakHashMap</code>, listeners). <strong>Phantom</strong> — never returns the object; used with <code>ReferenceQueue</code> for cleanup actions, modern alternative to <code>finalize()</code>.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How does the JIT compiler work? What is C1 vs C2?',
        a: 'JVM starts in interpreter mode. Hot methods (identified by invocation/loop counters) are JIT-compiled. <strong>C1</strong> (client) — fast compilation, simple optimizations, gathers profiling. <strong>C2</strong> (server) — aggressive optimization (inlining, escape analysis, loop unrolling) using C1\'s profile data. Tiered compilation uses both. Deoptimization happens when assumptions break (e.g., a new subclass loaded).' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What is escape analysis and what optimizations does it enable?',
        a: 'JIT analyzes whether an object\'s reference "escapes" its method scope. If it doesn\'t escape: <strong>stack allocation</strong> (sometimes called scalar replacement) — object fields become stack variables, no heap allocation. Also enables <strong>lock elision</strong> — synchronized blocks on non-escaping objects can be removed entirely. Massive win for short-lived objects.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How would you diagnose a production OutOfMemoryError?',
        a: '<ul><li>Add <code>-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=/path</code> to capture a dump.</li><li>Analyze with Eclipse MAT or VisualVM — look for dominator tree and largest retained heap.</li><li>Check GC logs (<code>-Xlog:gc*</code>) for frequency and pattern.</li><li>Common culprits: unbounded caches, ThreadLocal leaks, classloader leaks (after redeploys), large session objects.</li><li>Use <code>jmap -histo</code> and <code>jstat</code> for live inspection.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'Explain class loading and the parent delegation model.',
        a: 'Three default loaders: <strong>Bootstrap</strong> (JDK core), <strong>Platform/Extension</strong> (java.* extensions), <strong>Application</strong> (classpath). Parent delegation: a loader first asks its parent before loading itself — prevents core class spoofing. Tomcat/Spring Boot inverts this for app isolation (WebAppClassLoader loads itself first). Steps: Loading → Linking (verify, prepare, resolve) → Initialization (run <code>&lt;clinit&gt;</code>).' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between Minor GC and Major (Full) GC?',
        a: '<strong>Minor GC</strong> — collects only the Young Gen (Eden + Survivor). Frequent, fast (typically &lt;10ms), copies the few survivors out. <strong>Major/Full GC</strong> — collects Old Gen (and usually the entire heap). Rare, slower, longer pauses. Tuning aims to reduce promotion to Old Gen so Full GCs stay rare.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What do the -Xmx and -Xms JVM flags do?',
        a: '<code>-Xms</code> sets the <strong>initial</strong> heap size; <code>-Xmx</code> sets the <strong>maximum</strong>. Setting them equal avoids heap-resizing pauses and is recommended in containers. In Java 11+ inside containers, also use <code>-XX:MaxRAMPercentage</code> so the JVM respects the cgroup limit instead of seeing the host\'s RAM.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is Metaspace and how is it different from PermGen?',
        a: 'Metaspace (Java 8+) holds class metadata. Unlike the old PermGen (Java 7-): it lives in <strong>native memory</strong>, not the Java heap; it auto-resizes by default; OOM type is <code>OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace</code>. Cap it in production with <code>-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize</code> so a classloader leak doesn\'t consume all native memory.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What JDK tools come bundled for monitoring a JVM?',
        a: '<ul><li><code>jps</code> — list Java processes.</li><li><code>jstat</code> — GC stats over time.</li><li><code>jmap</code> — heap dumps and histograms.</li><li><code>jstack</code> — thread dumps (deadlock detection).</li><li><code>jcmd</code> — Swiss army knife (replaces several above).</li><li><code>jconsole</code>, <code>jvisualvm</code> (now external), <code>JFR</code> (Java Flight Recorder) — GUI/profiling.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What are the common types of OutOfMemoryError?',
        a: '<ul><li><code>Java heap space</code> — heap exhausted (most common).</li><li><code>GC overhead limit exceeded</code> — GC running constantly but reclaiming almost nothing.</li><li><code>Metaspace</code> — class metadata exhausted (often a classloader leak).</li><li><code>Direct buffer memory</code> — NIO off-heap exhausted.</li><li><code>unable to create new native thread</code> — thread limit hit (often a leak or unbounded pool).</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is Java bytecode and how can you inspect it?',
        a: 'Bytecode is the JVM\'s portable instruction set — what the compiler emits into <code>.class</code> files. Each instruction (push, invoke, store, branch) is a one-byte opcode plus operands. Inspect with <code>javap -c MyClass</code> (disassemble) or <code>javap -v MyClass</code> (verbose: constant pool, attributes). Tools like ASM and ByteBuddy let you generate/transform bytecode programmatically.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Can Java still have memory leaks even with garbage collection?',
        a: 'Yes — anything held by a strong reference is "live" to the GC even if conceptually dead. Common culprits: unbounded caches/maps, static collections that grow forever, listeners/callbacks never unregistered, <code>ThreadLocal</code>s not removed in pooled threads, <code>ClassLoader</code> leaks after redeploys (whole class graph retained). Diagnose via heap dump → MAT dominator tree.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is a heap dump and how do you analyze one?',
        a: 'A heap dump is a snapshot of all live objects at a moment in time (<code>.hprof</code> file). Capture with <code>-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError</code> on crash, or <code>jcmd &lt;pid&gt; GC.heap_dump</code> on demand. Analyze with Eclipse MAT — look at the <strong>dominator tree</strong> (what objects retain the most memory) and <strong>leak suspects report</strong> (auto-detected suspicious patterns).' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is method inlining and why does it matter?',
        a: 'The JIT replaces a method call with the method\'s body, eliminating the call overhead and unlocking further optimizations (constant folding, dead-code elimination, escape analysis). It\'s the single highest-value optimization. Small, frequently-called methods are inlined aggressively. Megamorphic call sites (many implementations) defeat inlining — keep hot interfaces small.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between throughput and pause time in GC tuning?',
        a: '<strong>Throughput</strong> = fraction of CPU time spent on application work (vs GC). High-throughput collectors (Parallel GC) tolerate longer pauses to maximize app work. <strong>Pause time</strong> = how long the app stops during GC. Low-pause collectors (G1, ZGC, Shenandoah) do most work concurrently. Pick by SLA: batch jobs prefer throughput; latency-sensitive APIs prefer pause time.' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'spring-core',
    name: 'Spring Core & IoC',
    desc: 'Beans, DI, lifecycle, configuration',
    questions: [
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is Inversion of Control (IoC) and Dependency Injection (DI)?',
        a: '<strong>IoC</strong> — instead of objects creating their dependencies, an external container provides them. <strong>DI</strong> is one implementation of IoC: the container "injects" dependencies via constructor, setter, or field. Spring\'s <code>ApplicationContext</code> is the IoC container. Result: loose coupling, easier testing, configurable wiring.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What are the main bean scopes in Spring?',
        a: '<ul><li><code>singleton</code> (default) — one instance per container.</li><li><code>prototype</code> — new instance every <code>getBean()</code> call.</li><li><code>request</code>, <code>session</code>, <code>application</code> — web-aware scopes.</li><li><code>websocket</code> — per WS session.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between @Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller?',
        a: 'All are <code>@Component</code> specializations — same registration behavior but different <strong>semantic intent</strong>. <code>@Repository</code> adds exception translation for persistence (converts vendor exceptions to <code>DataAccessException</code>). <code>@Controller</code> marks web controllers for handler mapping. <code>@Service</code> is purely semantic. Use the most specific one.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Compare constructor injection, setter injection, and field injection. Which is preferred?',
        a: '<strong>Constructor</strong> — preferred: enables immutability (<code>final</code> fields), fails fast if a dep is missing, no proxy issues, easy to test without Spring. <strong>Setter</strong> — for optional or reconfigurable deps. <strong>Field</strong> (<code>@Autowired</code> on field) — terse but hides dependencies, requires reflection in tests, and prevents <code>final</code>. Modern Spring recommends constructor injection exclusively.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain the Spring bean lifecycle.',
        a: '<ul><li>Instantiate → populate properties (DI) → call <code>*Aware</code> setters → <code>BeanPostProcessor.before</code> → <code>@PostConstruct</code> → <code>InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet</code> → custom <code>init-method</code> → <code>BeanPostProcessor.after</code></li><li>Bean is ready.</li><li>On shutdown: <code>@PreDestroy</code> → <code>DisposableBean.destroy</code> → custom <code>destroy-method</code>.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between BeanFactory and ApplicationContext?',
        a: '<code>BeanFactory</code> — basic IoC, lazy initialization. <code>ApplicationContext</code> extends it with: eager singleton pre-instantiation, event publication, internationalization, resource loading, AOP integration, and environment abstraction. In practice, you always use <code>ApplicationContext</code> (typically <code>AnnotationConfigApplicationContext</code> or <code>WebApplicationContext</code>).' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How does Spring resolve circular dependencies?',
        a: 'Spring resolves them for <strong>singleton beans with setter/field injection</strong> via a three-level cache: singletons → early references → factory objects. When bean A needs B and B needs A, Spring exposes a partially constructed A through the early-reference cache, lets B inject it, then completes A. <strong>Constructor injection</strong> cannot be resolved this way — Spring throws <code>BeanCurrentlyInCreationException</code>. Refactor or use <code>@Lazy</code>.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What happens when you inject a prototype bean into a singleton?',
        a: 'By default, the prototype is injected <strong>once</strong> at singleton creation and remains the same instance — defeating the purpose. Fixes: (1) inject <code>ObjectProvider&lt;T&gt;</code> or <code>Provider&lt;T&gt;</code> and call <code>get()</code> each time; (2) use <code>@Lookup</code> method injection — Spring overrides the method to return a fresh prototype; (3) inject the <code>ApplicationContext</code> and call <code>getBean()</code>.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What is the difference between @Configuration and @Component for defining beans?',
        a: 'Both can declare <code>@Bean</code> methods. <code>@Configuration</code> classes are wrapped by a <strong>CGLIB proxy</strong> so that intra-class <code>@Bean</code> calls return the singleton from the container (not a new instance each call). <code>@Component</code> classes don\'t proxy — calling <code>@Bean</code> methods creates new objects each time. Use <code>@Configuration</code> for config classes; <code>@Component</code> elsewhere.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'Explain how Spring AOP works under the hood.',
        a: 'Spring AOP uses <strong>runtime proxies</strong>: JDK dynamic proxies (if target implements an interface) or CGLIB subclass proxies (otherwise). When you call a proxied method, the proxy intercepts, runs the advice chain (around, before, after-returning, after-throwing), then delegates to the target. Limitations: only public method calls go through proxy; <strong>self-invocation</strong> (calling another method on <code>this</code>) bypasses advice. Use <code>AspectJ</code> compile-time weaving for full power.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What does @Autowired do?',
        a: 'Tells Spring to inject a matching bean from the container. Works on constructors (preferred), setters, fields. <code>required=false</code> makes the dependency optional (<code>null</code> if no match). With a single constructor since Spring 4.3, you can omit <code>@Autowired</code> entirely — constructor injection is implicit.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between @Bean and @Component?',
        a: '<code>@Component</code> declares a class as a Spring-managed bean and is discovered by component scanning. <code>@Bean</code> is a method-level annotation inside a <code>@Configuration</code> class — you control instantiation, useful for third-party classes you can\'t annotate (e.g., a JDBC <code>DataSource</code>) or when you need extra construction logic.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How does @Qualifier work and why do you need it?',
        a: 'When multiple beans of the same type exist, <code>@Autowired</code> alone is ambiguous. <code>@Qualifier("beanName")</code> picks one by name. Alternatives: name the parameter to match a bean name; use <code>@Primary</code> on one bean to make it default; or define a custom qualifier annotation for type-safe wiring.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is @Primary used for?',
        a: 'When multiple beans satisfy an injection point, the one marked <code>@Primary</code> is chosen as the default. Saves sprinkling <code>@Qualifier</code> everywhere. Common pattern: real implementation marked <code>@Primary</code>, a debug/mock implementation registered but not primary.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What does @Value do?',
        a: 'Injects values from the environment into fields or parameters: <code>@Value("${server.port}")</code> reads a property, <code>@Value("${app.timeout:30}")</code> supplies a default if missing. Supports SpEL: <code>@Value("#{systemProperties[\'user.home\']}")</code>. For grouped/typed config, prefer <code>@ConfigurationProperties</code> over many <code>@Value</code> fields.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What are the main ways to configure a Spring application?',
        a: '<ul><li><strong>XML</strong> — legacy, very explicit; rarely used today.</li><li><strong>Annotation-based</strong> — <code>@Component</code> + component scan; minimal config.</li><li><strong>Java config</strong> — <code>@Configuration</code> classes with <code>@Bean</code> methods; type-safe, refactor-friendly, recommended for non-trivial wiring.</li></ul>Modern apps mix annotations (for app code) and Java config (for infrastructure beans).' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between @PostConstruct and InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()?',
        a: 'Both run after dependency injection. <code>@PostConstruct</code> is a JSR-250 annotation — framework-agnostic, runs <em>before</em> <code>afterPropertiesSet()</code>. <code>InitializingBean</code> couples your code to Spring. Prefer <code>@PostConstruct</code> for portability and clarity; reserve the interface for framework-level code.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you load environment-specific properties using @Profile?',
        a: 'Place <code>application-dev.properties</code>, <code>application-prod.properties</code>, etc., alongside <code>application.properties</code>. Activate with <code>spring.profiles.active=prod</code> (property, env var, or CLI). Annotate beans with <code>@Profile("dev")</code> to register only under that profile. Multiple profiles can be active at once; profile-specific files override the base.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is SpEL and where can you use it?',
        a: 'Spring Expression Language — a small expression language for runtime values. Usable in <code>@Value("#{T(Math).random()}")</code>, <code>@PreAuthorize("hasRole(\'ADMIN\')")</code>, <code>@Cacheable(condition="#user.id != 0")</code>, XML config, and more. Supports property access, method invocation, collections, type references. Powerful but easy to abuse — keep expressions simple.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do BeanPostProcessor and BeanFactoryPostProcessor differ?',
        a: '<code>BeanFactoryPostProcessor</code> runs <strong>before</strong> any beans are instantiated — it can modify bean <em>definitions</em> (e.g., <code>PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer</code> resolves <code>${...}</code> placeholders). <code>BeanPostProcessor</code> runs <strong>after</strong> each bean is instantiated — it can wrap/replace beans (used by AOP, <code>@Async</code>, <code>@Autowired</code> processing). One operates on definitions, the other on instances.' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'spring-boot',
    name: 'Spring Boot & Auto-config',
    desc: 'Starters, auto-configuration, profiles, properties',
    questions: [
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What problem does Spring Boot solve compared to plain Spring?',
        a: 'Boilerplate elimination: (1) <strong>starter dependencies</strong> bundle compatible jar versions; (2) <strong>auto-configuration</strong> creates sensible default beans; (3) <strong>embedded server</strong> — runnable JAR with Tomcat/Jetty/Undertow; (4) <strong>opinionated defaults</strong> for properties, logging, health checks. Result: production-ready apps in minutes, not weeks of XML.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is a Spring Boot starter?',
        a: 'A curated dependency aggregator. <code>spring-boot-starter-web</code> pulls in Spring MVC, Jackson, Tomcat, validation, logging — all version-aligned. You add one dependency instead of managing dozens. Custom starters follow the <code>*-spring-boot-starter</code> naming convention.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you externalize configuration in Spring Boot?',
        a: 'Property sources in priority order (highest first): command-line args → JNDI → system properties → environment variables → profile-specific <code>application-{profile}.properties</code> → <code>application.properties</code> → <code>@PropertySource</code> → defaults. Bind via <code>@Value</code> or type-safe <code>@ConfigurationProperties</code>.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How does @SpringBootApplication work?',
        a: 'Meta-annotation combining <code>@Configuration</code> + <code>@EnableAutoConfiguration</code> + <code>@ComponentScan</code>. <code>@EnableAutoConfiguration</code> triggers auto-config loading from <code>META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports</code> (or legacy <code>spring.factories</code>). Scan starts from the package containing the main class — place it at the root.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How does Spring Boot auto-configuration actually decide what to configure?',
        a: 'Each auto-config class is gated by <strong>conditional annotations</strong>: <code>@ConditionalOnClass</code> (jar present), <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> (let user override), <code>@ConditionalOnProperty</code> (toggle via config), <code>@ConditionalOnWebApplication</code>, etc. Run with <code>--debug</code> to see the auto-config report showing matched and non-matched conditions.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you use Spring profiles?',
        a: 'Annotate beans with <code>@Profile("dev")</code> or define <code>application-dev.properties</code>. Activate via <code>spring.profiles.active=dev,monitoring</code> as property, env var (<code>SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE</code>), or CLI flag. Multiple profiles can be active. Use for environment-specific datasources, mocking external services, or feature toggles.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How would you write a custom auto-configuration?',
        a: '<ul><li>Create a <code>@Configuration</code> class with conditional annotations.</li><li>Add it to <code>META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports</code>.</li><li>Define <code>@ConfigurationProperties</code> for type-safe config.</li><li>Use <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> so users can override.</li><li>Order with <code>@AutoConfigureAfter</code> / <code>@AutoConfigureBefore</code>.</li><li>Package as a standalone JAR for reuse.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What is Spring Boot Actuator and what are its critical production endpoints?',
        a: 'Provides production-ready monitoring/management. Key endpoints: <code>/health</code> (liveness/readiness probes), <code>/metrics</code> (Micrometer — exports to Prometheus, Datadog), <code>/info</code>, <code>/loggers</code> (runtime log level changes), <code>/threaddump</code>, <code>/heapdump</code>, <code>/env</code>. <strong>Security warning</strong>: most should not be publicly exposed — limit via <code>management.endpoints.web.exposure.include</code> and a separate management port.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How does Spring Boot handle graceful shutdown?',
        a: 'Set <code>server.shutdown=graceful</code>. On SIGTERM, the server stops accepting new requests, waits up to <code>spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase</code> (default 30s) for in-flight requests, then closes. Critical for Kubernetes rolling deploys. Combine with <code>preStop</code> hook delays so the load balancer removes the pod before shutdown begins.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How would you optimize Spring Boot startup time?',
        a: '<ul><li>Use <strong>AOT compilation + GraalVM native image</strong> — sub-100ms startup.</li><li>Lazy initialization: <code>spring.main.lazy-initialization=true</code>.</li><li>Trim auto-configuration: exclude unused (<code>@SpringBootApplication(exclude={...})</code>).</li><li>Use <code>spring-context-indexer</code> to skip classpath scanning.</li><li>Profile with <code>-Dspring.context.indexer.enabled=true</code> and Spring Startup endpoint.</li><li>Reduce dependencies; avoid heavy starters in microservices.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between application.properties and application.yml?',
        a: 'Same data, different syntax. <code>.properties</code> is flat key=value; <code>.yml</code> is hierarchical YAML — cleaner for nested groups. Spring Boot accepts either (and SnakeYAML is bundled). Pick one per project to avoid confusion. YAML is preferred for any non-trivial config.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you change the default port of a Spring Boot app?',
        a: 'Set <code>server.port=8081</code> in <code>application.properties</code>, or pass it on the command line: <code>--server.port=8081</code>, or as env var <code>SERVER_PORT=8081</code>. Set to <code>0</code> for a random free port (useful in tests). Set to <code>-1</code> to disable the web server (e.g., for non-web Spring Boot apps).' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is @ConfigurationProperties?',
        a: 'Type-safe, structured binding of properties to a POJO: <code>@ConfigurationProperties(prefix="app")</code> binds all <code>app.*</code> keys to fields/setters. Combined with <code>@ConstructorBinding</code> (since Boot 3, default for record types) it supports immutability. Better than scattered <code>@Value</code> for any config group — also enables IDE autocomplete via the metadata processor.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you run code on Spring Boot startup?',
        a: 'Implement <code>CommandLineRunner</code> or <code>ApplicationRunner</code> as a bean — Boot invokes <code>run()</code> after the context starts, before reporting "Started". For lifecycle hooks: <code>@PostConstruct</code> on a bean, or listen to <code>ApplicationReadyEvent</code> (runs even later, after everything is fully up).' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is Spring Boot DevTools?',
        a: 'Dev-only dependency that adds: <strong>automatic restart</strong> when classpath changes (very fast — uses two classloaders), <strong>LiveReload</strong> in the browser, and dev-friendly property defaults (e.g., disabled template caching). Excluded automatically from production JARs. Add via <code>spring-boot-devtools</code> with <code>optional</code> scope.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you switch from Tomcat to Jetty or Undertow?',
        a: 'In Maven/Gradle, exclude <code>spring-boot-starter-tomcat</code> from <code>spring-boot-starter-web</code> and add <code>spring-boot-starter-jetty</code> or <code>spring-boot-starter-undertow</code>. Boot\'s auto-config picks up whichever embedded server is on the classpath. Each has slightly different config namespaces (e.g., <code>server.jetty.*</code>) — check the relevant docs.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How does logging work in Spring Boot by default?',
        a: 'Logback under SLF4J is the default (bundled via <code>spring-boot-starter-logging</code>). Configure via <code>logging.level.com.example=DEBUG</code> in properties, or drop in a custom <code>logback-spring.xml</code> for full control. To switch to Log4j2: exclude <code>spring-boot-starter-logging</code> and add <code>spring-boot-starter-log4j2</code>.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you use @SpringBootTest for integration tests?',
        a: '<code>@SpringBootTest</code> boots the full application context. Default web mode is <code>MOCK</code> (no real server); use <code>WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT</code> + <code>TestRestTemplate</code> / <code>WebTestClient</code> for real HTTP. Slice annotations (<code>@WebMvcTest</code>, <code>@DataJpaTest</code>) load only relevant beans — much faster than the full context.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you exclude a specific auto-configuration?',
        a: 'Three ways: (1) <code>@SpringBootApplication(exclude = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)</code>; (2) <code>spring.autoconfigure.exclude=...</code> in properties; (3) ensure the conditional gate fails (e.g., remove the triggering jar). Useful when an unwanted auto-config tries to wire a missing dependency.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is an executable JAR in Spring Boot, and how is it different from a regular JAR?',
        a: 'A Boot "fat" or "über" JAR contains your code, all dependencies, and a tiny launcher. Layout: <code>BOOT-INF/classes</code> (yours), <code>BOOT-INF/lib</code> (deps), <code>org/springframework/boot/loader</code> (launcher). <code>java -jar</code> runs the launcher, which uses a custom <code>ClassLoader</code> to load nested JARs. Build with <code>spring-boot-maven-plugin</code>\'s <code>repackage</code> goal.' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'spring-mvc',
    name: 'Spring MVC & REST APIs',
    desc: 'Controllers, mapping, validation, exceptions',
    questions: [
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between @Controller and @RestController?',
        a: '<code>@Controller</code> — returns view names resolved by a <code>ViewResolver</code> (for server-rendered HTML). <code>@RestController</code> = <code>@Controller</code> + <code>@ResponseBody</code> on every method — return values are serialized directly (usually to JSON via Jackson). Use <code>@RestController</code> for REST APIs.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What does @RequestMapping do? Compare to @GetMapping, @PostMapping, etc.',
        a: '<code>@RequestMapping</code> is the generic mapping — supports all methods, configurable via <code>method</code> attribute. The HTTP-specific ones (<code>@GetMapping</code>, <code>@PostMapping</code>, <code>@PutMapping</code>, <code>@DeleteMapping</code>, <code>@PatchMapping</code>) are shortcuts introduced in Spring 4.3. Prefer the specific ones — clearer intent, less verbose.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you extract path variables, query params, and request body?',
        a: '<ul><li><code>@PathVariable</code> — URI template segment: <code>/users/{id}</code>.</li><li><code>@RequestParam</code> — query string or form: <code>?page=1</code>.</li><li><code>@RequestBody</code> — deserializes request body (e.g., JSON) into an object.</li><li><code>@RequestHeader</code> — header values.</li><li><code>@CookieValue</code> — cookie values.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you implement validation in a REST controller?',
        a: 'Annotate DTO fields with Bean Validation (<code>@NotNull</code>, <code>@Email</code>, <code>@Size</code>, <code>@Min</code>, etc.). Add <code>@Valid</code> on the <code>@RequestBody</code> parameter. Spring throws <code>MethodArgumentNotValidException</code> on failure. Handle globally with <code>@RestControllerAdvice</code> + <code>@ExceptionHandler</code> to return structured 400 responses. For custom rules: implement <code>ConstraintValidator</code>.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you implement global exception handling?',
        a: 'Create a class annotated with <code>@RestControllerAdvice</code> (or <code>@ControllerAdvice</code>). Add methods with <code>@ExceptionHandler(SomeException.class)</code> that return <code>ResponseEntity</code>. You can scope advice with <code>basePackages</code> or <code>assignableTypes</code>. Since Spring 6, prefer <code>ProblemDetail</code> (RFC 7807) for error responses.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is HATEOAS? Should you use it?',
        a: 'Hypermedia As The Engine Of Application State — responses include links to related actions/resources, enabling clients to navigate the API dynamically. Spring HATEOAS adds <code>EntityModel</code>, <code>RepresentationModel</code>, <code>Link</code>. <strong>Pragmatic view</strong>: full HATEOAS is rare in practice — most teams ship simpler JSON. Use when API discoverability matters (e.g., a developer portal) or when implementing HAL/JSON-API specs.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'Explain the full DispatcherServlet request flow.',
        a: '<ul><li>Request → <code>DispatcherServlet</code> (front controller).</li><li><code>HandlerMapping</code> resolves to handler (controller method).</li><li><code>HandlerAdapter</code> invokes the handler; <code>HandlerInterceptor.preHandle</code> runs.</li><li>Arguments resolved by <code>HandlerMethodArgumentResolver</code>; return value handled by <code>HandlerMethodReturnValueHandler</code>.</li><li>Response written via <code>HttpMessageConverter</code> (e.g., <code>MappingJackson2HttpMessageConverter</code>).</li><li><code>postHandle</code> → view resolution if applicable → <code>afterCompletion</code>.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How does content negotiation work in Spring?',
        a: 'Spring picks an <code>HttpMessageConverter</code> based on the <code>Accept</code> header. Default strategy checks (1) URL suffix (<code>.json</code>, deprecated in 5.3+), (2) <code>Accept</code> header, (3) <code>format</code> query param if configured. Configure with <code>ContentNegotiationConfigurer</code>. Same applies to <code>Content-Type</code> for incoming requests via <code>consumes</code> attribute.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How do you implement pagination and sorting in a REST API?',
        a: 'Accept a <code>Pageable</code> parameter — Spring binds from <code>?page=0&size=20&sort=createdAt,desc</code>. Repository methods accept <code>Pageable</code> and return <code>Page&lt;T&gt;</code>. Response can use <code>PagedModel</code> (Spring HATEOAS) or a custom envelope: <code>{"content":[...], "totalElements":N, "totalPages":N, "page":N}</code>. Always cap max page size to prevent abuse.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How do you handle file uploads and streaming responses?',
        a: '<strong>Upload</strong>: <code>@RequestPart("file") MultipartFile file</code> or <code>@RequestParam</code>. Configure <code>spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size</code>. <strong>Streaming</strong>: return <code>StreamingResponseBody</code>, <code>ResponseEntity&lt;Resource&gt;</code> (e.g., <code>InputStreamResource</code>), or <code>SseEmitter</code> for server-sent events. For very large files use chunked transfer; consider WebFlux for high-concurrency streaming.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What HTTP status codes should a REST API return for common operations?',
        a: '<ul><li><code>200 OK</code> — successful GET/PUT/PATCH/DELETE with body.</li><li><code>201 Created</code> — successful POST creating a resource (include <code>Location</code> header).</li><li><code>204 No Content</code> — success with no body (often DELETE).</li><li><code>400 Bad Request</code> — validation/parse error.</li><li><code>401</code>/<code>403</code> — unauthenticated/forbidden.</li><li><code>404</code> — not found.</li><li><code>409</code> — conflict (duplicate, optimistic lock).</li><li><code>422</code> — semantic validation error.</li><li><code>500</code> — server bug.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between PUT and PATCH?',
        a: '<code>PUT</code> — full replacement; the request body contains the entire resource. Idempotent. <code>PATCH</code> — partial update; the body describes only the changes (JSON Patch, JSON Merge Patch, or your own format). Not necessarily idempotent. Most APIs use PATCH for "update some fields" to avoid forcing clients to send the whole object.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What does @ResponseBody do, and why does @RestController not need it?',
        a: '<code>@ResponseBody</code> tells Spring to serialize the return value into the response body (via <code>HttpMessageConverter</code>s — usually JSON) instead of resolving it as a view name. <code>@RestController</code> is <code>@Controller + @ResponseBody</code>, so it\'s applied to every method automatically.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you enable CORS in a Spring REST API?',
        a: 'Three options: (1) <code>@CrossOrigin</code> on a controller or method for ad-hoc rules; (2) <code>WebMvcConfigurer.addCorsMappings(registry)</code> for app-wide config; (3) for Spring Security apps, add a <code>CorsConfigurationSource</code> bean and call <code>http.cors(Customizer.withDefaults())</code>. Don\'t set <code>allowedOrigins("*")</code> together with <code>allowCredentials(true)</code> — the browser will reject it.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is @ModelAttribute used for?',
        a: 'Two roles: (1) <strong>method parameter</strong> — binds form fields/query params to an object (Spring instantiates and populates via setters); (2) <strong>method-level</strong> — pre-populates the model for every handler in the controller. Mostly used in server-rendered MVC; in REST APIs, prefer <code>@RequestBody</code>.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you customize JSON serialization with Jackson in Spring?',
        a: 'A few layers: (1) annotations on the DTO — <code>@JsonProperty</code>, <code>@JsonIgnore</code>, <code>@JsonFormat</code>; (2) <code>application.properties</code> — <code>spring.jackson.date-format</code>, <code>spring.jackson.default-property-inclusion=non_null</code>; (3) global override — provide a <code>Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilderCustomizer</code> bean; (4) per-controller — register a custom <code>MappingJackson2HttpMessageConverter</code>. For different shapes per endpoint, use <code>@JsonView</code>.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is a HandlerInterceptor and how is it different from a Filter?',
        a: 'Both intercept requests. <strong>Filter</strong> — servlet API, runs <em>outside</em> the Spring MVC stack; sees raw <code>HttpServletRequest</code>. Use for cross-cutting concerns like compression, CORS, security. <strong>HandlerInterceptor</strong> — Spring MVC-specific; runs <em>inside</em> <code>DispatcherServlet</code>; has access to the handler method and model. Use for app-level concerns (logging request mappings, auditing).' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What are common approaches to REST API versioning?',
        a: '<ul><li><strong>URI</strong>: <code>/v1/users</code> — easy, very visible.</li><li><strong>Header</strong>: <code>Accept: application/vnd.acme.v2+json</code> — clean URLs but opaque.</li><li><strong>Query param</strong>: <code>?version=2</code> — easy to test, but URLs become inconsistent.</li><li><strong>No versioning</strong> — evolve additively (never remove or rename) and use feature toggles.</li></ul>URI versioning is the pragmatic default for public APIs.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is ResponseEntity and when should you use it?',
        a: '<code>ResponseEntity&lt;T&gt;</code> is a wrapper that lets you set status code, headers, and body explicitly: <code>ResponseEntity.status(201).header("Location", uri).body(created)</code>. Use it when you need anything beyond the default 200 + body — created resources, conditional GETs (<code>304</code>), or custom headers. For plain success responses, returning the body directly is cleaner.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you handle 404 (no handler found) globally?',
        a: 'Two prerequisites: <code>spring.mvc.throw-exception-if-no-handler-found=true</code> and <code>spring.web.resources.add-mappings=false</code> (otherwise the static resource handler swallows it). Then a <code>@ExceptionHandler(NoHandlerFoundException.class)</code> on a <code>@RestControllerAdvice</code> can return a clean JSON 404. Spring 6+ <code>ProblemDetail</code> + RFC 7807 is the modern way.' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'data-jpa',
    name: 'Spring Data JPA & Hibernate',
    desc: 'Repositories, queries, transactions, performance',
    questions: [
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between JPA, Hibernate, and Spring Data JPA?',
        a: '<strong>JPA</strong> — specification (interfaces, annotations). <strong>Hibernate</strong> — most popular JPA implementation, plus extra features. <strong>Spring Data JPA</strong> — Spring layer on top: repository abstraction, derived queries, pagination, auditing. You write interfaces, Spring generates implementations at runtime.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What does the @Entity annotation require?',
        a: 'A no-arg constructor (any access level), a primary key field marked with <code>@Id</code>, and a non-final class. Optional: <code>@Table</code> for custom table name. Should override <code>equals()</code>/<code>hashCode()</code> based on a business key (not <code>@Id</code> if it\'s auto-generated, to avoid identity issues with transient entities).' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you define a derived query method?',
        a: 'By method naming convention on a <code>JpaRepository</code> interface: <code>List&lt;User&gt; findByLastNameAndAgeGreaterThan(String lastName, int age)</code>. Supported keywords: <code>findBy</code>, <code>And</code>, <code>Or</code>, <code>Between</code>, <code>LessThan</code>, <code>Like</code>, <code>OrderBy</code>, <code>Top</code>, <code>Distinct</code>. For complex queries use <code>@Query</code>.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain the JPA entity lifecycle states.',
        a: '<ul><li><strong>Transient/New</strong> — created with <code>new</code>, not associated with persistence context.</li><li><strong>Managed/Persistent</strong> — attached, changes auto-tracked and flushed.</li><li><strong>Detached</strong> — was managed but context closed; changes not tracked. Reattach with <code>merge()</code>.</li><li><strong>Removed</strong> — marked for deletion, will be DELETE on flush.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the N+1 query problem and how do you fix it?',
        a: 'Lazy-loading a collection: 1 query for parents, then N queries for each child collection. Example: <code>orders.forEach(o -&gt; o.getItems().size())</code> on 100 orders → 101 queries. <strong>Fixes</strong>: (1) <code>JOIN FETCH</code> in JPQL; (2) <code>@EntityGraph</code> on repository method; (3) <code>FetchType.EAGER</code> (avoid as default — too eager); (4) Hibernate <code>@BatchSize</code>; (5) DTO projection that queries only needed fields.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between save(), saveAndFlush(), and persist()?',
        a: '<code>save()</code> — Spring Data: <code>merge()</code> if entity has an ID (detached check), else <code>persist()</code>. Returns possibly new instance. <code>saveAndFlush()</code> — same plus immediate <code>flush()</code> to DB (useful for triggers, returning generated values). <code>persist()</code> — JPA: makes transient entity managed; fails if already detached.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'Explain @Transactional propagation levels.',
        a: '<ul><li><code>REQUIRED</code> (default) — join existing or start new.</li><li><code>REQUIRES_NEW</code> — always start a new transaction, suspending the outer one.</li><li><code>SUPPORTS</code> — join if exists, run non-transactionally otherwise.</li><li><code>MANDATORY</code> — fail if no transaction.</li><li><code>NEVER</code> — fail if transaction exists.</li><li><code>NESTED</code> — savepoint within outer (Spring JDBC, not all JPA providers).</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'Why doesn\'t self-invocation of @Transactional methods work?',
        a: 'Spring AOP uses a proxy. External callers go through the proxy → advice → target. But calling <code>this.someMethod()</code> within the same class bypasses the proxy entirely, so the transactional advice never runs. <strong>Fixes</strong>: (1) inject self via <code>@Autowired</code> and call through it; (2) move the transactional method to another bean; (3) use AspectJ weaving instead of proxy-based AOP.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What is optimistic vs pessimistic locking?',
        a: '<strong>Optimistic</strong> — <code>@Version</code> field; on update, JPA adds <code>WHERE version = ?</code>; failure throws <code>OptimisticLockException</code>. No DB locks held. Best for low contention. <strong>Pessimistic</strong> — <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> via <code>@Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)</code>; DB-level row locks; safe under heavy contention but reduces throughput and can deadlock.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How would you optimize a slow Spring Data JPA query?',
        a: '<ul><li>Enable SQL logging: <code>spring.jpa.show-sql=true</code> + <code>logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG</code>.</li><li>Check execution plan with <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> — verify indexes.</li><li>Replace lazy iteration with <code>JOIN FETCH</code> or <code>@EntityGraph</code>.</li><li>Use DTO projection (constructor expression or interface) — fewer columns, no entity overhead.</li><li>Add pagination — never load unbounded result sets.</li><li>Enable second-level cache or query cache for hot reads.</li><li>For bulk operations: <code>JdbcTemplate</code> batch update bypasses persistence context.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What does @GeneratedValue do, and what are the strategies?',
        a: 'Tells JPA to auto-generate the primary key. Strategies: <code>AUTO</code> — provider picks; <code>IDENTITY</code> — DB auto-increment (one INSERT, but blocks JDBC batching); <code>SEQUENCE</code> — DB sequence (best for PostgreSQL/Oracle, supports batching); <code>TABLE</code> — emulates a sequence in a table (avoid). Prefer <code>SEQUENCE</code> where supported, or <code>IDENTITY</code> for MySQL.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between FetchType.LAZY and EAGER?',
        a: '<strong>EAGER</strong> — associated entities loaded immediately with the parent. <strong>LAZY</strong> — loaded only when the field is accessed (via a proxy). Defaults: <code>@ManyToOne</code>/<code>@OneToOne</code> are EAGER; <code>@OneToMany</code>/<code>@ManyToMany</code> are LAZY. <strong>Best practice</strong>: make everything LAZY and use <code>JOIN FETCH</code> / <code>@EntityGraph</code> for the specific queries that need it.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What does @Transactional do?',
        a: 'Marks a method (or class) to run inside a database transaction. Spring opens a transaction before the method, commits if it returns normally, rolls back on a <strong>runtime exception</strong> (checked exceptions don\'t trigger rollback by default — use <code>rollbackFor</code>). Bound to the current thread; supports propagation, isolation, timeout, and read-only hints.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between findById() and getReferenceById()?',
        a: '<code>findById()</code> — runs a <code>SELECT</code>, returns <code>Optional&lt;Entity&gt;</code> (or empty if missing). <code>getReferenceById()</code> — returns a <strong>proxy</strong> without hitting the DB; throws <code>EntityNotFoundException</code> later if accessed and missing. Useful when you only need the ID for a foreign-key reference: <code>order.setUser(userRepo.getReferenceById(userId))</code>.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you write a custom JPQL query with @Query?',
        a: '<code>@Query("select u from User u where u.email = :email")</code> on a repository method, parameters as <code>:name</code> bind to method args (with <code>@Param("email")</code> if compiled without parameter names). For native SQL add <code>nativeQuery = true</code>. For updates: <code>@Modifying</code> + <code>@Query("update ...")</code> inside a transaction.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain CascadeType options and when to use orphanRemoval.',
        a: 'CascadeType propagates persistence operations from parent to children: <code>PERSIST</code>, <code>MERGE</code>, <code>REMOVE</code>, <code>REFRESH</code>, <code>DETACH</code>, or <code>ALL</code>. <code>orphanRemoval=true</code> deletes a child when it\'s removed from the parent\'s collection. Use cascade for true ownership (Order → OrderItems); don\'t cascade across aggregates (User → Posts) — handle those explicitly.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the persistence context and how does it work?',
        a: 'A session-scoped first-level cache of managed entities. Inside one transaction, looking up the same ID returns the same instance (identity guarantee). Changes are tracked via <strong>dirty checking</strong> — JPA compares snapshots at flush time and issues UPDATEs only for changed fields. The context flushes before queries (so writes are visible) and at commit.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do JPA relationships work — @OneToMany, @ManyToOne, @ManyToMany?',
        a: 'In SQL: <code>@ManyToOne</code> = foreign key on this table; <code>@OneToMany</code> = inverse side (no FK on this side, use <code>mappedBy</code>); <code>@ManyToMany</code> = join table. Always set <code>mappedBy</code> on the inverse side to avoid duplicate FKs. For <code>@ManyToMany</code>, consider promoting to two <code>@OneToMany</code>s with an explicit join entity once you need extra columns.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between JPQL and native SQL queries?',
        a: '<strong>JPQL</strong> — object-oriented; queries entities and their fields (<code>select u from User u</code>); portable across databases; auto-maps to entities. <strong>Native SQL</strong> — raw SQL; database-specific syntax allowed; needed for DB-specific features (window functions, UPSERT, FULLTEXT). Mapping native results to entities is fiddly — use DTO projections or <code>@SqlResultSetMapping</code>.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is dirty checking in Hibernate?',
        a: 'When a transaction is about to commit (or flush), Hibernate compares each managed entity\'s current state against the snapshot taken at load time, and issues UPDATEs only for the entities that changed. Side effect: simply mutating a managed entity persists the change — no <code>save()</code> required. Watch out: modifying a detached entity won\'t flush; reattach with <code>merge()</code>.' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'security',
    name: 'Spring Security',
    desc: 'Authentication, authorization, JWT, OAuth2',
    questions: [
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between authentication and authorization?',
        a: '<strong>Authentication</strong> — who are you? (verifying identity via password, token, certificate). <strong>Authorization</strong> — what are you allowed to do? (role/permission check). Spring Security: <code>AuthenticationManager</code> handles the first, <code>AccessDecisionManager</code>/<code>AuthorizationManager</code> the second.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the role of SecurityFilterChain?',
        a: 'A chain of servlet filters applied to every request before reaching the controller. Each filter handles a concern: CSRF, CORS, authentication, authorization, exception translation. The filter order matters — Spring Security provides sensible defaults. Customize via <code>SecurityFilterChain</code> bean in modern (5.7+) config.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you hash passwords in Spring Security?',
        a: 'Use <code>PasswordEncoder</code>. Default and recommended: <code>BCryptPasswordEncoder</code> — adaptive, salted. Modern alternative: <code>Argon2PasswordEncoder</code>. Never store plaintext or use MD5/SHA-1. Use <code>DelegatingPasswordEncoder</code> (default in Boot) which encodes a prefix like <code>{bcrypt}</code> in the hash — enables algorithm migration.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain stateless JWT authentication flow.',
        a: '<ul><li>Client posts credentials → server validates → returns signed JWT.</li><li>Client stores JWT (memory, httpOnly cookie); sends as <code>Authorization: Bearer ...</code> on each request.</li><li>Server validates signature + expiration on every request — no session needed.</li><li>Stateless: any node can handle any request. Trade-off: revocation is hard (use short expiry + refresh tokens, or maintain a blocklist).</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is CSRF and how does Spring Security protect against it?',
        a: 'Cross-Site Request Forgery — malicious site triggers a state-changing request using the victim\'s session cookie. Spring Security generates a token, embeds it in forms (<code>_csrf</code>), and validates on POST/PUT/DELETE. <strong>Stateless JWT APIs</strong> can disable CSRF — no session cookie means no automatic credential replay. Keep enabled for browser-based session apps.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you implement role and permission-based authorization?',
        a: 'At URL level: <code>http.authorizeHttpRequests(a -&gt; a.requestMatchers("/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN"))</code>. At method level: <code>@PreAuthorize("hasRole(\'ADMIN\')")</code> or <code>@PreAuthorize("hasAuthority(\'USER_DELETE\')")</code>. Enable with <code>@EnableMethodSecurity</code>. Roles are authorities prefixed with <code>ROLE_</code>. Prefer fine-grained permissions for flexibility.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'Walk through the Spring Security authentication architecture.',
        a: '<ul><li>Request hits <code>UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter</code> (or <code>BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter</code> for JWT).</li><li>Filter creates an unauthenticated <code>Authentication</code> object.</li><li>Passes to <code>AuthenticationManager</code> (usually <code>ProviderManager</code>).</li><li>Provider iterates <code>AuthenticationProvider</code>s — e.g., <code>DaoAuthenticationProvider</code> loads via <code>UserDetailsService</code>, checks password via <code>PasswordEncoder</code>.</li><li>On success, returns authenticated <code>Authentication</code> → stored in <code>SecurityContextHolder</code>.</li><li>On failure, <code>AuthenticationEntryPoint</code> handles the response (401, redirect to login).</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What\'s the difference between OAuth2 and OpenID Connect?',
        a: '<strong>OAuth2</strong> — authorization framework: grants access tokens for delegated access to resources. Doesn\'t define identity. <strong>OIDC</strong> — identity layer on top of OAuth2: adds <code>id_token</code> (JWT), <code>userinfo</code> endpoint, standard claims (sub, email, name). Use OIDC when you need to know <em>who</em> the user is; OAuth2 alone when you only need API access.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How would you implement refresh tokens with Spring Security?',
        a: '<ul><li>Issue both at login: short-lived access token (5–15 min), long-lived refresh token (days).</li><li>Store refresh token server-side (DB or Redis) — must be revocable.</li><li>Client calls <code>/refresh</code> endpoint with refresh token → server validates, issues new pair (rotation prevents reuse).</li><li>Add device/IP binding and revocation on suspicious activity.</li><li>Detect reuse — if a revoked refresh token is presented, revoke entire family (token theft signal).</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How do you secure a microservices architecture with Spring Security?',
        a: '<ul><li><strong>Gateway pattern</strong>: API Gateway authenticates JWT, propagates user context downstream via headers.</li><li><strong>Service-to-service</strong>: mTLS or signed JWTs (OAuth2 client credentials flow).</li><li>Each service validates JWT signature locally using JWKS from auth server — no auth server round-trips.</li><li>Use OAuth2 resource server config: <code>http.oauth2ResourceServer(o -&gt; o.jwt())</code>.</li><li>Centralize policies via OPA or dedicated authz service for complex rules.</li><li>Service mesh (Istio) can offload TLS/authn entirely.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between basic auth and form login?',
        a: '<strong>Basic auth</strong> — credentials in every request via <code>Authorization: Basic base64(user:pass)</code>; browser shows a native dialog. Stateless, simple, no logout concept. <strong>Form login</strong> — POST to <code>/login</code>, server creates a session and returns a cookie. Better UX (custom page, logout, remember-me). Use basic for service-to-service or quick prototypes; form login for human-facing apps.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is in SecurityContext and SecurityContextHolder?',
        a: '<code>SecurityContext</code> holds the current <code>Authentication</code> object (principal + authorities). <code>SecurityContextHolder</code> stores it — by default in a <code>ThreadLocal</code> (per-request thread). Spring Security clears it after each request. For async code, you may need <code>SecurityContextHolderStrategy.MODE_INHERITABLETHREADLOCAL</code> or explicit propagation.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you configure in-memory users for testing?',
        a: 'Expose an <code>InMemoryUserDetailsManager</code> bean built from <code>User.withUsername("u").password("{bcrypt}...").roles("USER").build()</code>. Always prefix the password with an encoder ID (<code>{bcrypt}</code>, <code>{noop}</code> for tests only). Useful for local dev and integration tests — never for production.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is UserDetailsService?',
        a: 'The interface Spring Security uses to look up user data by username (one method: <code>loadUserByUsername</code>, returns <code>UserDetails</code> with password, authorities, account flags). Implement it to load users from your DB. The password encoder verifies the supplied password against the stored hash.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is a GrantedAuthority?',
        a: 'A simple permission string attached to an authenticated user — e.g., <code>"ROLE_ADMIN"</code>, <code>"USER_DELETE"</code>. The authorization layer checks these via <code>hasRole()</code>, <code>hasAuthority()</code>, or <code>@PreAuthorize</code>. Roles are just authorities with a <code>ROLE_</code> prefix — <code>hasRole("ADMIN")</code> checks for <code>ROLE_ADMIN</code>.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between hasRole() and hasAuthority()?',
        a: '<code>hasRole("ADMIN")</code> matches the authority <code>ROLE_ADMIN</code> (the prefix is added automatically). <code>hasAuthority("ROLE_ADMIN")</code> matches the exact string — useful for non-role authorities like <code>USER_DELETE</code>. If you skip the role concept entirely and grant fine-grained permissions, use <code>hasAuthority</code>.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you customize the login page in Spring Security?',
        a: 'Override form login config: <code>http.formLogin(f -&gt; f.loginPage("/login").permitAll().defaultSuccessUrl("/").failureUrl("/login?error"))</code>. Then build the <code>/login</code> view (any template engine) with a POST form to the configured login-processing URL. For REST APIs, replace form login with a JSON-based authentication filter.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between an authentication failure handler and an access denied handler?',
        a: '<strong>AuthenticationEntryPoint</strong> / failure handler — runs when the user is <em>not authenticated</em> (no/invalid credentials). For browsers: redirect to login. For APIs: return 401. <strong>AccessDeniedHandler</strong> — runs when the user IS authenticated but lacks the required authority. For browsers: show 403 page. For APIs: return 403.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How would you implement remember-me functionality?',
        a: 'Built-in support: <code>http.rememberMe(r -&gt; r.key("uniqueSecret").tokenValiditySeconds(86400 * 30))</code>. Two strategies: <strong>hash-based</strong> — token = hash(username, password, expiry, key); simple, but invalidates if the password changes. <strong>persistent</strong> — store a token in DB (<code>JdbcTokenRepositoryImpl</code>); supports per-device revocation. Always serve the cookie as <code>Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax</code>.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What common security headers does Spring Security add, and what do they do?',
        a: '<ul><li><code>X-Frame-Options: DENY</code> — clickjacking protection.</li><li><code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code> — prevent MIME sniffing.</li><li><code>Strict-Transport-Security</code> (HTTPS only) — force HTTPS.</li><li><code>Cache-Control: no-cache</code> on protected pages.</li><li><code>Referrer-Policy</code> — control referer leakage.</li></ul>Configure via <code>http.headers(h -&gt; h.contentSecurityPolicy(cs -&gt; cs.policyDirectives("default-src \'self\'")))</code>.' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'spring-multithreading',
    name: 'Multithreading in Spring',
    desc: 'Async, scheduling, executors, virtual threads, async controllers',
    questions: [
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the default request-handling model in Spring MVC?',
        a: 'Spring MVC runs on a servlet container (Tomcat by default). Each incoming HTTP request is assigned <strong>one thread</strong> from the container\'s thread pool (Tomcat default: 200), and your controller code runs synchronously on that thread until it returns. Concurrency comes from many requests being served on different threads in parallel. Tune the pool with <code>server.tomcat.threads.max</code> and <code>server.tomcat.threads.min-spare</code>.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>@RestController\nclass HelloController {\n  @GetMapping("/hi")\n  String hi() {\n    return "served by " + Thread.currentThread().getName();\n    // → http-nio-8080-exec-1, http-nio-8080-exec-2, ...\n  }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is @Async and how do you enable it?',
        a: 'Annotate a method with <code>@Async</code> and Spring runs it on a separate thread, returning immediately to the caller. Enable it once with <code>@EnableAsync</code> on a <code>@Configuration</code> class. The method must be invoked through a Spring proxy (so external call), must be <code>public</code>, and must return <code>void</code> or a <code>Future</code>/<code>CompletableFuture</code>.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>@Configuration\n@EnableAsync\nclass AsyncConfig { }\n\n@Service\nclass EmailService {\n  @Async\n  public CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt; send(String to) {\n    smtpClient.send(to);\n    return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(null);\n  }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is ThreadPoolTaskExecutor?',
        a: 'Spring\'s wrapper around <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code>. Lets you configure core size, max size, queue capacity, thread name prefix, rejection policy, and graceful shutdown. Used as the default executor for <code>@Async</code>. Always prefer it over <code>Executors.newFixedThreadPool(...)</code> in a Spring app — Spring manages its lifecycle and exposes Micrometer metrics.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>@Bean("taskExecutor")\npublic ThreadPoolTaskExecutor taskExecutor() {\n  var ex = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();\n  ex.setCorePoolSize(8);\n  ex.setMaxPoolSize(32);\n  ex.setQueueCapacity(500);\n  ex.setThreadNamePrefix("async-");\n  ex.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());\n  ex.initialize();\n  return ex;\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is @Scheduled and how do you enable it?',
        a: 'Marks a method to run on a schedule. Enable with <code>@EnableScheduling</code>. Supports <code>fixedRate</code> (start times spaced N ms apart), <code>fixedDelay</code> (N ms after each completion), <code>cron</code> (cron expression), and <code>initialDelay</code>. Methods must be no-args and return <code>void</code>. By default, all scheduled tasks share a single-threaded scheduler — override by exposing a <code>TaskScheduler</code> bean.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>@Configuration\n@EnableScheduling\nclass ScheduleConfig { }\n\n@Component\nclass Cleanup {\n  @Scheduled(fixedDelay = 60_000)        // 60s after last finishes\n  public void purgeOldFiles() { ... }\n\n  @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 3 * * *")       // 3:00 AM daily\n  public void nightlyReport() { ... }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you configure the Tomcat thread pool in Spring Boot?',
        a: 'In <code>application.properties</code>: <code>server.tomcat.threads.max=400</code>, <code>server.tomcat.threads.min-spare=20</code>, <code>server.tomcat.max-connections=10000</code>, <code>server.tomcat.accept-count=200</code>. Threads handle active requests; <code>max-connections</code> caps total open sockets; <code>accept-count</code> queues connections beyond that. For Jetty/Undertow use the equivalent <code>server.jetty.*</code> / <code>server.undertow.*</code> namespaces.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code># application.properties\nserver.tomcat.threads.max=400\nserver.tomcat.threads.min-spare=20\nserver.tomcat.max-connections=10000\nserver.tomcat.accept-count=200\nserver.tomcat.connection-timeout=20s</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What\'s the difference between void and CompletableFuture return types for @Async?',
        a: '<code>void</code> — fire-and-forget; the caller has no way to know when it finishes or whether it threw. <code>CompletableFuture&lt;T&gt;</code> (or <code>Future&lt;T&gt;</code>) — the caller can call <code>.get()</code>, chain with <code>thenApply</code>/<code>thenCompose</code>, and observe exceptions. Prefer <code>CompletableFuture</code> for composable, traceable workflows; reserve <code>void</code> for true fire-and-forget like sending an email.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// Fire-and-forget — caller has no handle\n@Async void logEvent(Event e) { ... }\n\n// Composable — caller can chain and observe failures\n@Async\nCompletableFuture&lt;Report&gt; buildReport(int id) {\n  return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(new Report(...));\n}\n\n// Caller side\nbuildReport(42)\n  .thenApply(this::render)\n  .exceptionally(ex -&gt; { log.error("failed", ex); return null; });</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'How do you set thread name prefixes for better debugging?',
        a: 'On a <code>ThreadPoolTaskExecutor</code>: <code>executor.setThreadNamePrefix("email-")</code>. Resulting threads are named <code>email-1</code>, <code>email-2</code>, etc. Critical in production — thread dumps and logs become far easier to read when each pool has a distinctive prefix.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>@Bean("emailExecutor")\nThreadPoolTaskExecutor emailExecutor() {\n  var ex = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();\n  ex.setCorePoolSize(4);\n  ex.setThreadNamePrefix("email-");   // threads: email-1, email-2, ...\n  ex.initialize();\n  return ex;\n}\n// In jstack output: "email-3" runnable [...] — easy to grep for</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is a TaskDecorator and when do you need one?',
        a: '<code>TaskDecorator</code> wraps every <code>Runnable</code> submitted to a Spring executor so you can run code before/after the task on the worker thread. The classic use: <strong>propagating ThreadLocal context</strong> (SecurityContext, MDC, RequestContext) from the caller\'s thread to the worker — those <code>ThreadLocal</code>s don\'t cross threads automatically.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>public class MdcTaskDecorator implements TaskDecorator {\n  @Override public Runnable decorate(Runnable task) {\n    var ctx = MDC.getCopyOfContextMap();   // capture on caller thread\n    return () -&gt; {\n      try {\n        if (ctx != null) MDC.setContextMap(ctx);\n        task.run();\n      } finally {\n        MDC.clear();\n      }\n    };\n  }\n}\n\n@Bean ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor() {\n  var ex = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();\n  ex.setTaskDecorator(new MdcTaskDecorator());\n  ex.initialize();\n  return ex;\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Why does @Async self-invocation not work?',
        a: 'Spring implements <code>@Async</code> via a <strong>proxy</strong>. External callers go through the proxy → advice → target method (runs on a thread pool). But calling <code>this.someAsync()</code> inside the same class bypasses the proxy entirely, so the async advice never runs and the method executes synchronously on the caller\'s thread. <strong>Fixes</strong>: inject the bean into itself (<code>@Lazy</code> self-reference), move the async method to another bean, or use AspectJ compile-time weaving.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// BROKEN: direct \'this\' call — proxy bypassed, runs synchronously\n@Service\nclass Notifier {\n  public void notifyAll(List&lt;User&gt; users) {\n    users.forEach(this::sendOne);\n  }\n  @Async public void sendOne(User u) { ... }\n}\n\n// FIX: inject self via @Lazy so the call goes through the proxy\n@Service\nclass Notifier {\n  @Autowired @Lazy Notifier self;\n  public void notifyAll(List&lt;User&gt; users) {\n    users.forEach(self::sendOne);   // → proxy → @Async kicks in\n  }\n  @Async public void sendOne(User u) { ... }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you enable virtual threads in Spring Boot 3.2+?',
        a: 'Single property: <code>spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true</code> (requires Java 21+). Tomcat, <code>@Async</code>, <code>@Scheduled</code>, and many other Spring components automatically switch to virtual threads. Result: tens of thousands of concurrent blocking I/O calls become cheap. Caveat: <code>synchronized</code> blocks during I/O still pin the carrier thread (improving in Java 23/24).<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code># application.properties — Java 21 required\nspring.threads.virtual.enabled=true\n\n// That\'s it. Now each request runs on a virtual thread:\n@GetMapping("/orders/{id}")\nOrder get(@PathVariable long id) {\n  return orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n  // ↑ blocking JDBC — carrier thread is released during the wait,\n  //   so thousands of concurrent requests cost almost nothing\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you handle exceptions thrown from @Async void methods?',
        a: '<code>void</code> methods can\'t return the exception. Spring drops it unless you register an <code>AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler</code>. Implement <code>AsyncConfigurer.getAsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler()</code> — your handler receives the exception, method name, and arguments so you can log/alert/retry. For methods returning <code>CompletableFuture</code>, exceptions surface through <code>.exceptionally(...)</code> or <code>.get()</code>.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>@Configuration\n@EnableAsync\nclass AsyncConfig implements AsyncConfigurer {\n  @Override\n  public AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler getAsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler() {\n    return (ex, method, params) -&gt;\n      log.error("uncaught in {}({})", method.getName(), params, ex);\n  }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass Worker {\n  @Async void doWork() {\n    throw new RuntimeException("oops");  // → goes to handler above\n  }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you propagate SecurityContext, MDC, and RequestContext to @Async threads?',
        a: 'These live in <code>ThreadLocal</code>s — they don\'t cross threads. Three options: (1) custom <code>TaskDecorator</code> on your executor that snapshots and restores them; (2) wrap your executor with <code>DelegatingSecurityContextAsyncTaskExecutor</code> for SecurityContext; (3) for MDC: snapshot <code>MDC.getCopyOfContextMap()</code> in the decorator. With virtual threads in Boot 3.2+, scoped values (<code>ScopedValue</code>) are an emerging cleaner alternative.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// Option 1: SecurityContext only — Spring-provided wrapper\n@Bean ThreadPoolTaskExecutor base() { /* ... */ return ex; }\n\n@Bean AsyncTaskExecutor secureExecutor(ThreadPoolTaskExecutor base) {\n  return new DelegatingSecurityContextAsyncTaskExecutor(base);\n}\n\n// Option 2: All three with a custom TaskDecorator\nclass ContextDecorator implements TaskDecorator {\n  public Runnable decorate(Runnable r) {\n    var sec = SecurityContextHolder.getContext();\n    var mdc = MDC.getCopyOfContextMap();\n    var req = RequestContextHolder.getRequestAttributes();\n    return () -&gt; {\n      SecurityContextHolder.setContext(sec);\n      if (mdc != null) MDC.setContextMap(mdc);\n      RequestContextHolder.setRequestAttributes(req);\n      try { r.run(); }\n      finally {\n        SecurityContextHolder.clearContext();\n        MDC.clear();\n        RequestContextHolder.resetRequestAttributes();\n      }\n    };\n  }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you wire multiple TaskExecutors and select one per @Async call?',
        a: 'Declare multiple beans, then reference one by name: <code>@Async("emailExecutor")</code> or <code>@Async("reportExecutor")</code>. This is the right pattern for <strong>bulkheading</strong> — slow downstream service A gets its own pool so it can\'t starve service B\'s pool. Without a name, Spring uses the bean named <code>"taskExecutor"</code> or falls back to <code>SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor</code> (a new thread per call — almost never what you want in production).<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>@Bean("emailExecutor")\nTaskExecutor email() { /* small dedicated pool */ }\n\n@Bean("reportExecutor")\nTaskExecutor report() { /* larger pool for heavy work */ }\n\n@Service\nclass Notifier {\n  @Async("emailExecutor")  void send(User u) { ... }\n  @Async("reportExecutor") CompletableFuture&lt;Report&gt; build(int id) { ... }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Why doesn\'t @Transactional propagate to @Async methods, and how do you handle it?',
        a: 'Transactions are bound to the <strong>current thread</strong> via <code>ThreadLocal</code>. When <code>@Async</code> jumps to a worker thread, the transaction doesn\'t come along. Two fixes: (1) annotate the async method itself with <code>@Transactional</code> — Spring opens a fresh transaction on the worker thread; (2) pass entity IDs (not entities) to the async method and reload inside the new transaction. Never share managed JPA entities across threads.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// BROKEN: outer @Transactional doesn\'t reach the async thread\n@Transactional\npublic void process(Order o) {\n  charge(o);\n  asyncEmail(o);          // runs on another thread, NO transaction\n}\n\n@Async public void asyncEmail(Order o) {\n  emailRepo.save(...);    // not transactional!\n}\n\n// FIX: annotate the async method itself, pass IDs not entities\n@Async @Transactional\npublic void asyncEmail(long orderId) {\n  Order o = orderRepo.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();\n  emailRepo.save(...);\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What are the async return types for Spring MVC controllers?',
        a: '<ul><li><code>Callable&lt;T&gt;</code> — Spring runs it on its async executor; releases the Tomcat thread until done.</li><li><code>DeferredResult&lt;T&gt;</code> — you complete it from another thread (e.g., a callback); great for long-polling.</li><li><code>CompletableFuture&lt;T&gt;</code> — return one and Spring waits asynchronously.</li><li><code>StreamingResponseBody</code> — stream a long response body without buffering.</li><li><code>SseEmitter</code> — server-sent events.</li><li><code>ResponseBodyEmitter</code> — generic async emitter.</li></ul>All free up the request thread while you wait.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// Callable — Spring runs on its async executor\n@GetMapping("/a")\nCallable&lt;Report&gt; a() { return () -&gt; buildReport(); }\n\n// DeferredResult — completed from another thread\n@GetMapping("/b")\nDeferredResult&lt;String&gt; b() {\n  var dr = new DeferredResult&lt;String&gt;(5_000L);\n  worker.submit(() -&gt; dr.setResult(doWork()));\n  return dr;\n}\n\n// CompletableFuture — Spring waits asynchronously\n@GetMapping("/c")\nCompletableFuture&lt;Order&gt; c(long id) { return orderService.findAsync(id); }\n\n// SseEmitter — server-sent events stream\n@GetMapping("/events")\nSseEmitter events() {\n  var em = new SseEmitter();\n  worker.submit(() -&gt; { em.send("hello"); em.complete(); });\n  return em;\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you size a thread pool for CPU-bound vs I/O-bound workloads?',
        a: '<strong>CPU-bound</strong> (encryption, image processing): pool size ≈ number of CPU cores + 1. More threads just thrash the scheduler. <strong>I/O-bound</strong> (HTTP calls, DB queries): pool size ≈ <code>cores × (1 + wait_time / compute_time)</code> — often 50–200 for typical microservices. Measure: track queue depth and utilization via Micrometer. <strong>Virtual threads</strong> remove most of this math for I/O-bound work — use an unbounded virtual-thread executor instead.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>int cores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();\n\n// CPU-bound: cores + 1\n@Bean("cpuExecutor")\nThreadPoolTaskExecutor cpu() {\n  var ex = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();\n  ex.setCorePoolSize(cores + 1);\n  ex.setMaxPoolSize(cores + 1);\n  ex.initialize();\n  return ex;\n}\n\n// I/O-bound: large pool, work mostly waits\n@Bean("ioExecutor")\nThreadPoolTaskExecutor io() {\n  var ex = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();\n  ex.setCorePoolSize(50);\n  ex.setMaxPoolSize(200);\n  ex.setQueueCapacity(1000);\n  ex.initialize();\n  return ex;\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What are ThreadPoolExecutor rejection policies and when do you use each?',
        a: 'When the queue is full and pool is at max size, the executor calls a <code>RejectedExecutionHandler</code>. Built-in policies:<ul><li><code>AbortPolicy</code> (default) — throws <code>RejectedExecutionException</code>. Safe default; surfaces overload to upstream.</li><li><code>CallerRunsPolicy</code> — caller thread runs the task itself, naturally back-pressuring producers. Good for ingestion pipelines.</li><li><code>DiscardPolicy</code> — silently drop. Only OK for metrics/lossy work.</li><li><code>DiscardOldestPolicy</code> — drop the head of the queue and try again. Useful when newer data dominates (heartbeats, telemetry).</li></ul>For most Spring services: AbortPolicy + a circuit breaker upstream.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.ThreadPoolExecutor.*;\n\nvar ex = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();\nex.setCorePoolSize(8);\nex.setMaxPoolSize(32);\nex.setQueueCapacity(500);\n\n// pick ONE of the following:\nex.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new AbortPolicy());        // default: throws\nex.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new CallerRunsPolicy());   // backpressure\nex.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new DiscardOldestPolicy()); // freshest wins\nex.setRejectedExecutionHandler(new DiscardPolicy());      // lossy\n\nex.initialize();</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'When should you pick WebFlux/Reactor over MVC with virtual threads?',
        a: 'Pre-Java 21, WebFlux was the answer for handling tens of thousands of concurrent I/O-bound connections on a small thread pool. With virtual threads (Boot 3.2+, Java 21), MVC + JDBC + virtual threads achieves similar throughput with familiar imperative code. <strong>Pick WebFlux when</strong>: you need true backpressure (slow consumer signals upstream), reactive streams across the network (RSocket, SSE at scale), or your stack is end-to-end non-blocking (R2DBC, WebClient). <strong>Pick MVC + virtual threads when</strong>: most of the team thinks imperative, you have blocking dependencies (JDBC, legacy SDKs), or debuggability matters more than backpressure semantics.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// MVC + virtual threads (Boot 3.2+): blocking code that scales\n@RestController\nclass OrderApi {\n  @GetMapping("/orders/{id}")\n  Order get(@PathVariable long id) {\n    return repo.findById(id).orElseThrow();   // blocking JDBC — fine\n  }\n}\n\n// WebFlux: reactive end-to-end (needs R2DBC, WebClient, etc.)\n@RestController\nclass OrderApi {\n  @GetMapping("/orders/{id}")\n  Mono&lt;Order&gt; get(@PathVariable long id) {\n    return repo.findById(id);                 // non-blocking R2DBC\n  }\n}</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How do you handle thread pool exhaustion in a Spring microservice?',
        a: '<ul><li><strong>Bulkhead</strong> — separate <code>TaskExecutor</code> per downstream dependency so one slow service can\'t starve others.</li><li><strong>Timeouts</strong> — every blocking call must have a deadline (<code>WebClient</code>, <code>RestTemplate</code> + <code>HttpClient</code>, JDBC <code>statement_timeout</code>).</li><li><strong>Circuit breaker</strong> — Resilience4j fails fast when downstream is unhealthy, sparing your pool.</li><li><strong>Bounded queues</strong> — never use unbounded; rejection is feedback, not failure.</li><li><strong>Observability</strong> — alert on thread pool saturation and queue depth (Micrometer exposes both).</li><li><strong>Long-term</strong> — switch I/O-heavy paths to virtual threads (Boot 3.2+).</li></ul><div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// One executor per dependency — slow downstream A can\'t starve B\n@Bean("apiAExecutor") TaskExecutor a() { /* size 10 */ }\n@Bean("apiBExecutor") TaskExecutor b() { /* size 10 */ }\n\n// Circuit breaker fails fast on a sick downstream\n@CircuitBreaker(name = "apiA", fallbackMethod = "apiAFallback")\n@Bulkhead(name = "apiA")\nString callA() {\n  return webClientA.get()\n    .retrieve().bodyToMono(String.class)\n    .block(Duration.ofSeconds(2));   // always a timeout\n}\n\nString apiAFallback(Throwable t) { return "cached"; }</code></pre>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What pitfalls come with using parallelStream() in a Spring app?',
        a: '<code>parallelStream()</code> uses the <strong>shared common ForkJoinPool</strong> — the same pool used by <code>CompletableFuture.supplyAsync()</code> (default executor) and parallel streams in every other bean. Risks: (1) one slow task starves every other parallel-stream user across the app; (2) default pool size is <code>cores - 1</code> — tiny for I/O-bound work; (3) <code>ThreadLocal</code> context (SecurityContext, MDC, transactions) doesn\'t propagate; (4) ordering and exception handling become tricky. <strong>Better</strong>: submit work to a named, sized <code>ThreadPoolTaskExecutor</code> via <code>CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(task, executor)</code>.<div class="ex-label">Example</div><pre><code>// BAD: uses the shared common ForkJoinPool — starvation risk\norders.parallelStream()\n  .map(this::callExternalApi)\n  .forEach(this::save);\n\n// GOOD: dedicated executor, explicit lifecycle, context propagation\n@Autowired @Qualifier("ioExecutor") Executor ioExec;\n\nList&lt;CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt;&gt; futures = orders.stream()\n  .map(o -&gt; CompletableFuture.runAsync(\n      () -&gt; save(callExternalApi(o)), ioExec))\n  .toList();\n\nCompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(CompletableFuture[]::new)).join();</code></pre>' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'microservices',
    name: 'Microservices Patterns',
    desc: 'Service discovery, resilience, distributed tx',
    questions: [
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What are microservices and what problem do they solve?',
        a: 'Architectural style: an app is a suite of small, independently deployable services, each owning a business capability and its data. Solves monolith pain: long build/deploy cycles, single tech stack, scaling bottlenecks, team coordination. Trade-off: massive operational complexity — only worth it past a certain team/system scale.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is service discovery?',
        a: 'Mechanism for services to find each other dynamically (no hardcoded URLs). Two styles: <strong>client-side</strong> (Netflix Eureka — client queries registry, picks instance); <strong>server-side</strong> (Kubernetes Service, AWS ELB — client calls a fixed endpoint, infra routes). Kubernetes DNS + Service is now the dominant pattern.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is an API Gateway?',
        a: 'Single entry point for client traffic. Handles: routing, authentication, rate limiting, request/response transformation, aggregation across services, SSL termination, logging. Examples: Spring Cloud Gateway, Kong, AWS API Gateway, Envoy. Prevents clients from knowing every service URL and centralizes cross-cutting concerns.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain the circuit breaker pattern.',
        a: 'Wraps remote calls to prevent cascading failures. States: <strong>Closed</strong> (calls pass through, errors counted) → <strong>Open</strong> (after threshold, calls fail fast for a cooldown) → <strong>Half-Open</strong> (trial calls; on success → Closed, on failure → Open). Implementation: <strong>Resilience4j</strong> (Spring Cloud Circuit Breaker). Combine with timeouts, retries, and fallbacks.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you handle distributed transactions across microservices?',
        a: 'Avoid two-phase commit — too brittle. Use the <strong>Saga pattern</strong>: a sequence of local transactions, each publishing an event that triggers the next. On failure, run compensating transactions to undo previous steps. Two flavors: <strong>Choreography</strong> (events, no central coordinator) and <strong>Orchestration</strong> (central orchestrator — e.g., Camunda, Temporal). Orchestration is easier to debug.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the outbox pattern and what problem does it solve?',
        a: 'Solves the <strong>dual-write problem</strong>: writing to the DB and publishing to a message broker atomically. Solution: write the message to an <strong>outbox</strong> table in the <em>same DB transaction</em> as the business data. A separate process (CDC like Debezium, or polling) reads the outbox and publishes to the broker, then marks as sent. Guarantees at-least-once delivery without distributed transactions.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How would you design distributed tracing?',
        a: 'Each request gets a <code>traceId</code> (entire journey) and per-hop <code>spanId</code>. Propagate via headers (W3C Trace Context: <code>traceparent</code>). Instrument with <strong>Micrometer Tracing</strong> (Spring Boot 3) → exports to <strong>Zipkin/Jaeger/Tempo</strong>. View as a flame graph showing where latency lives. Critical for diagnosing performance in multi-hop calls. Pair with correlation IDs in logs.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'How do you handle eventual consistency in a microservices system?',
        a: '<ul><li><strong>Accept it.</strong> Strong consistency across services is rarely worth the cost.</li><li>Use event-driven updates — services subscribe to changes they care about.</li><li>Idempotent consumers — same event arriving twice has no side effect (dedup table or natural idempotency).</li><li>Read models / CQRS — denormalized views per use case, eventually consistent.</li><li>Compensating actions for inevitable conflicts (e.g., refund if double-charged).</li><li>UX: surface "processing" states, optimistic updates.</li></ul>' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'When would you choose synchronous (REST/gRPC) vs asynchronous (messaging) communication?',
        a: '<strong>Sync</strong> — query operations needing immediate response, tight coupling acceptable, simple flows. Risk: latency adds up, failures cascade. <strong>Async</strong> — fire-and-forget, fan-out events, long-running work, services with independent SLAs, smoothing load spikes. Costs: harder to reason about, requires idempotency and event versioning. In practice: hybrid — sync for reads, async for state changes that fan out.' },
      { d: 'hard', q: 'What is the bulkhead pattern?',
        a: 'Isolate resources so a failure in one area doesn\'t exhaust shared resources elsewhere — like watertight compartments in a ship. <strong>Thread pool bulkhead</strong>: separate executor per downstream service (slow service A doesn\'t starve service B). <strong>Semaphore bulkhead</strong>: cap concurrent calls per dependency. Resilience4j supports both. Combine with circuit breakers and timeouts for full resilience.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the difference between monolith, modular monolith, and microservices?',
        a: '<strong>Monolith</strong> — one deployable, one DB, easy to develop and deploy. <strong>Modular monolith</strong> — still one deployable, but enforced module boundaries (clear APIs between modules). <strong>Microservices</strong> — many deployables, each owns its data. Start with a modular monolith. Split into microservices only when team or operational pain justifies the complexity tax.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is a configuration server in Spring Cloud?',
        a: 'A centralized service that serves configuration to other services — backed by Git, Vault, or a database. Services fetch their config at startup (and optionally refresh at runtime via <code>@RefreshScope</code>). Benefits: rotate secrets without redeploy; promote config through environments via Git; single source of truth.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is a health check and why does it matter?',
        a: 'An endpoint a load balancer/orchestrator hits to decide if your service is healthy enough to receive traffic. Spring Boot Actuator exposes <code>/actuator/health</code> with <strong>liveness</strong> (is the process alive?) and <strong>readiness</strong> (can it serve traffic right now?) probes. Critical for Kubernetes — unhealthy pods are restarted (liveness) or removed from Service endpoints (readiness).' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the BFF (Backend For Frontend) pattern?',
        a: 'Per-client backend that aggregates and adapts data for one specific UI (web BFF, mobile BFF). Solves: different clients want different shapes/fields; mobile cares about payload size; web cares about specific aggregations. Avoids polluting upstream services with UI concerns. Trade-off: one more service to maintain per client.' },
      { d: 'easy', q: 'What is service-to-service authentication, and why do you need it?',
        a: 'Without it, any service in the cluster can call any other — a single compromised service exposes everything. Common approaches: <strong>mTLS</strong> (both sides present certs; often via a service mesh like Istio), <strong>signed JWTs</strong> (OAuth2 client-credentials), <strong>API keys</strong> per caller. Always combine with network policies that restrict which pods can talk to which.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the strangler fig pattern?',
        a: 'Incremental migration from a legacy system: put a routing layer in front, peel off one piece of functionality at a time into the new system, route only that traffic to the new code, repeat. After enough cycles, the legacy system "withers" and can be removed. Lower risk than a big-bang rewrite — each step is shippable and reversible.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you implement retries safely?',
        a: '<ul><li>Only retry <strong>idempotent</strong> operations (or operations with an idempotency key).</li><li>Use <strong>exponential backoff + jitter</strong> — uniform retries cause thundering herds.</li><li>Cap retry count and total budget; don\'t retry forever.</li><li>Distinguish retryable errors (transient: timeouts, 5xx, connection reset) from non-retryable (4xx).</li><li>Combine with a circuit breaker to fail fast when downstream is dead.</li></ul>Resilience4j and Spring Retry both implement this.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between blue-green and canary deployment?',
        a: '<strong>Blue-green</strong> — run two full production stacks; flip 100% of traffic at once from blue → green; rollback by flipping back. Simple but expensive (double the infra). <strong>Canary</strong> — route a small fraction of traffic (1%, 5%, 25%) to the new version; observe metrics; gradually shift. Catches issues with real traffic before full rollout. Often combined with feature flags.' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between orchestration and choreography in event-driven systems?',
        a: '<strong>Orchestration</strong> — a central component (e.g., Temporal, Camunda, a saga orchestrator) tells each service what to do next. Easy to reason about, easy to monitor, but the orchestrator becomes a coupling point. <strong>Choreography</strong> — each service reacts to events from others; no central brain. More decoupled but harder to trace and debug ("why did this happen?").' },
      { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you implement idempotency in HTTP APIs?',
        a: 'Client sends an <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header (UUID) on POST/PATCH. Server stores <code>(key, response)</code> in Redis with a TTL (24h is common). Same key replay → return the stored response, do not re-execute. Combine with: (1) a DB constraint on a business key to prevent duplicates if the cache is missed; (2) document the policy clearly in the API spec.' }
    ]
  }
];

const extras = {
  id: 'extras',
  name: 'Extras — MQ, Redis, Docker, K8s',
  desc: 'Adjacent systems Java engineers are expected to know',
  questions: [
    { d: 'easy', q: '[Redis] What data types does Redis support and when would you use each?',
      a: '<ul><li><strong>String</strong> — caching, counters, session data.</li><li><strong>List</strong> — queues, recent items (capped lists).</li><li><strong>Hash</strong> — object fields without serializing whole object.</li><li><strong>Set</strong> — unique items, tags, membership tests.</li><li><strong>Sorted Set (ZSet)</strong> — leaderboards, time-series, priority queues.</li><li><strong>Stream</strong> — event log with consumer groups (Kafka-lite).</li><li><strong>Bitmap, HyperLogLog, Geo</strong> — specialized use cases.</li></ul>' },
    { d: 'easy', q: '[MQ] What are the differences between Kafka and RabbitMQ?',
      a: '<strong>Kafka</strong> — distributed log, partitioned, retains messages (days/forever), pull-based, optimized for high throughput and replayability. Consumers track offsets. <strong>RabbitMQ</strong> — traditional message broker (AMQP), push-based, routes via exchanges, deletes after ack, rich routing rules. Use Kafka for event streaming/analytics/event sourcing; RabbitMQ for task queues, RPC, complex routing.' },
    { d: 'easy', q: '[Docker] What is the difference between an image and a container?',
      a: '<strong>Image</strong> — immutable template, layered filesystem, built from a Dockerfile. <strong>Container</strong> — running instance of an image with its own writable layer, isolated namespaces (PID, network, mount). Many containers from one image. Images are versioned/tagged; containers are ephemeral.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '[Redis] Explain cache patterns: cache-aside, write-through, write-behind.',
      a: '<ul><li><strong>Cache-aside (lazy)</strong> — app reads cache; on miss, reads DB and populates cache. App writes go to DB; cache is invalidated. Most common.</li><li><strong>Write-through</strong> — app writes to cache, cache writes synchronously to DB. Slower writes, consistent reads.</li><li><strong>Write-behind (write-back)</strong> — app writes to cache; cache flushes to DB async. Fast but risk of data loss on cache crash.</li></ul>' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '[Redis] How do you prevent cache penetration, breakdown, and avalanche?',
      a: '<ul><li><strong>Penetration</strong> (queries for non-existent keys hit DB): cache null values briefly; use Bloom filter to pre-check existence.</li><li><strong>Breakdown</strong> (hot key expires → thundering herd to DB): mutex/lock around DB load; logical expiration (refresh in background).</li><li><strong>Avalanche</strong> (many keys expire simultaneously): randomize TTLs; use a multi-tier cache; circuit breaker on DB.</li></ul>' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '[MQ] What is the difference between at-most-once, at-least-once, and exactly-once delivery?',
      a: '<strong>At-most-once</strong> — fire-and-forget, may lose messages. Fast, simple. <strong>At-least-once</strong> — retry until acked, may duplicate. Most common; requires idempotent consumers. <strong>Exactly-once</strong> — no loss, no duplicates. Achieved by combining at-least-once + idempotency (transactional outbox + consumer dedup, or Kafka transactional producer + EOS).' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '[K8s] What are Pods, Deployments, and Services?',
      a: '<strong>Pod</strong> — smallest deployable unit, one or more containers sharing network/storage. <strong>Deployment</strong> — manages ReplicaSets which manage Pods; handles rolling updates and rollbacks. <strong>Service</strong> — stable network endpoint (ClusterIP/NodePort/LoadBalancer) that load-balances to Pods matched by labels. Pods come and go; Services are the address.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: '[Kafka] How do you ensure ordering and exactly-once processing in Kafka?',
      a: '<ul><li><strong>Ordering</strong>: guaranteed within a partition only. Use a partition key (e.g., userId) so all related messages land in the same partition. Single consumer per partition.</li><li><strong>EOS (exactly-once semantics)</strong>: producer-side <code>enable.idempotence=true</code> + transactional writes; consumer-side <code>isolation.level=read_committed</code>. For Kafka Streams: <code>processing.guarantee=exactly_once_v2</code>.</li><li>Cross-system EOS still needs idempotent sinks.</li></ul>' },
    { d: 'hard', q: '[Redis] How would you implement a distributed lock with Redis? What are the pitfalls?',
      a: 'Basic: <code>SET key value NX PX 30000</code> — atomic set-if-not-exists with TTL. Release with a Lua script checking value matches. <strong>Pitfalls</strong>: (1) TTL too short → another holder thinks lock is free; (2) clock skew between nodes; (3) GC pause holding lock past expiration. <strong>Redlock</strong> (Antirez): acquire on majority of N independent Redis nodes. Controversial — Martin Kleppmann argues fencing tokens are needed. For critical correctness, use Zookeeper/etcd instead.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: '[K8s] Explain liveness, readiness, and startup probes.',
      a: '<ul><li><strong>Liveness</strong> — is the app alive? Fail → kubelet restarts the container. Use for deadlock detection. Conservative thresholds.</li><li><strong>Readiness</strong> — is the app ready to serve traffic? Fail → Pod removed from Service endpoints (no restart). Use for warm-up, dependency checks.</li><li><strong>Startup</strong> — for slow-starting apps. Disables liveness/readiness until success. Prevents premature restarts during boot. Spring Boot 2.3+ exposes <code>/actuator/health/liveness</code> and <code>/readiness</code> natively.</li></ul>' },
    { d: 'hard', q: '[System] How would you design a high-throughput rate limiter?',
      a: '<ul><li><strong>Algorithms</strong>: token bucket (smooth bursts), leaky bucket (constant rate), fixed window (simple, edge bursts), sliding window log/counter (accurate, more memory).</li><li><strong>Storage</strong>: Redis with Lua scripts for atomic check-and-decrement — sub-millisecond. Local cache for read-heavy keys.</li><li><strong>Distribution</strong>: per-user limits via key hashing; global limits via centralized counter.</li><li><strong>Failure</strong>: fail open or fail closed? Usually fail open for non-critical limits.</li><li>Surface limits via <code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code> headers; respond 429 with <code>Retry-After</code>.</li></ul>' },
    { d: 'hard', q: '[System] How would you design a notification system handling 100M users?',
      a: '<ul><li><strong>Ingest</strong>: REST + Kafka topic per channel (email/push/SMS).</li><li><strong>Fan-out</strong>: worker pool per channel, scales horizontally on consumer groups.</li><li><strong>Templating + personalization</strong>: separate service; cache user preferences in Redis.</li><li><strong>Provider abstraction</strong>: pluggable adapters (SES, SNS, FCM, Twilio); circuit breakers per provider.</li><li><strong>Idempotency</strong>: dedup by (userId, notificationId) in Redis with TTL.</li><li><strong>Throttling</strong>: per-user, per-channel, and global; respect quiet hours.</li><li><strong>Delivery tracking</strong>: webhook receivers update status; analytics via separate pipeline.</li><li><strong>Backpressure</strong>: DLQ for failed sends; retry with exponential backoff.</li></ul>' },
    { d: 'easy', q: '[Redis] What is the difference between TTL and persistence?',
      a: '<strong>TTL</strong> (time-to-live) — per-key expiration set with <code>EXPIRE key seconds</code> or <code>SET ... EX seconds</code>. Redis lazily/proactively deletes expired keys. <strong>Persistence</strong> — how Redis writes the whole dataset to disk for recovery (RDB snapshots and/or AOF). Independent concerns: TTL controls per-key lifetime, persistence controls survival across restarts.' },
    { d: 'easy', q: '[Docker] What is the difference between COPY and ADD in a Dockerfile?',
      a: '<code>COPY</code> — copies local files into the image. Straightforward, predictable. <strong>Prefer this.</strong> <code>ADD</code> — also handles two extras: auto-extracts local <code>.tar</code> archives and fetches remote URLs. Those features are surprising and brittle (no checksum verification on URLs). Best practice: use <code>COPY</code> for files and <code>curl</code> + <code>RUN</code> for downloads (with checksums).' },
    { d: 'easy', q: '[K8s] What is the difference between Deployment, StatefulSet, and DaemonSet?',
      a: '<ul><li><strong>Deployment</strong> — stateless replicas, interchangeable pods, rolling updates. Default choice for web apps.</li><li><strong>StatefulSet</strong> — pods get stable identities (<code>app-0</code>, <code>app-1</code>) and persistent volumes. For DBs, Kafka brokers, anything needing stable storage/network.</li><li><strong>DaemonSet</strong> — one pod per node. For node-level agents (log shippers, monitoring).</li></ul>' },
    { d: 'easy', q: '[MQ] What is a dead letter queue (DLQ)?',
      a: 'A queue that receives messages a consumer failed to process after N retries. Keeps the main queue moving (poison-pill messages don\'t block it) while letting you investigate the failures later. Always set up alerting on DLQ depth — silent DLQs are how serious bugs hide.' },
    { d: 'easy', q: '[Docker] What does docker-compose do?',
      a: 'Defines multi-container apps declaratively in a YAML file: each service (image, ports, env, volumes, dependencies, networks). <code>docker compose up</code> brings the whole stack up; <code>down</code> tears it down. Great for local dev with realistic dependencies (app + DB + Redis + MQ). Not a production orchestrator — for that, use Kubernetes or ECS.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '[Redis] What persistence options does Redis offer (RDB vs AOF)?',
      a: '<strong>RDB</strong> — point-in-time snapshot every N seconds. Compact, fast restart, but you can lose up to N seconds of writes. <strong>AOF</strong> (append-only file) — log every write; replay on restart. Durable (configurable: every write, every second, OS-decided) but larger and slower restart. <strong>Best practice</strong>: enable both — AOF for durability, RDB for fast restore.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '[K8s] What is the difference between ConfigMap and Secret?',
      a: 'Both inject config into pods as env vars or mounted files. <strong>ConfigMap</strong> — plain key/value for non-sensitive config. <strong>Secret</strong> — for sensitive data (passwords, tokens, keys); stored base64-encoded by default (NOT encrypted at rest unless you enable encryption providers). For real secret management, use an external store (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager) and pull into pods at startup.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '[Docker] What are multi-stage builds and why use them?',
      a: 'A Dockerfile with multiple <code>FROM</code> stages — the final image copies only the artifacts it needs from earlier stages. Example: build stage uses the full JDK + Maven, runtime stage uses a slim JRE and just <code>COPY --from=build target/app.jar</code>. Drastically smaller images, smaller attack surface, faster pulls.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '[K8s] What is Ingress, and how does it differ from a Service?',
      a: '<strong>Service</strong> — internal L4 load balancer that exposes pods on a cluster-internal IP (or NodePort/LoadBalancer for external). <strong>Ingress</strong> — L7 HTTP routing: hostnames, paths, TLS termination, all consolidated behind a single external IP. Implemented by an Ingress controller (NGINX, Traefik, AWS ALB). Use Ingress instead of one LoadBalancer per service.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '[Kafka] What is a consumer group?',
      a: 'A set of consumers sharing a <code>group.id</code> that cooperatively read a topic — Kafka assigns each partition to exactly one consumer in the group, so each message is processed once per group. Scale parallelism by adding consumers (up to the partition count). Different groups read independently — same data, multiple subscribers.' }
  ]
};